var WIKI=[{"t": "Exile RPG Wiki", "u": "index.html", "c": "Getting started", "s": "The front page - what Exile RPG is, where to begin, and every page of the wiki.", "h": "Welcome, Stranded Start here Getting started Classes and stats Gear and the forge", "x": "Welcome, Stranded Exile RPG turns Minecraft Java into a full role-playing game. You wake up as an exile at a small camp on the edge of a hostile world. From there you: train your stats by using them - fighting, sprinting, blocking, mining, fishing and more, climb character levels , choose a class and grow it through its perk tree , hunt Magic, Rare and Champion monsters for rolled gear, orbs, gems and coins, take on Warden Aldric's story , the Quest Board and daily bounties , forge your own gear, ride mounts , earn titles , and team up in a party . Everything runs through the Exile's Journal and clickable chat menus. You never need to type a command. Start here First steps - your first hour, in order. The Exile's Journal - your character sheet and every menu. Leveling - how character levels work and what they give you. Quests - Warden Aldric, the Quest Board, bounties and the quest log. Classes - pick one at character level 5. Gear - how gear drops, levels and rarity. Stuck? Try the FAQ . Getting started Page What it covers First steps Your first hour, step by step The Exile's Journal The character sheet and every button on it Leveling Character level, character XP, what each level gives Coins and the bank Gold Coins, Gold Bars, selling, spending and the bank Quests Story quests, the Quest Board, bounties, the quest log Parties Making a party and what it shares Mounts Hilde's stable, the Rider's Whistle, upgrades Titles Achievements and the titles you can wear Death and safety Dying, what you keep, no PvP FAQ Quick answers to common questions Classes and stats Page What it covers Classes Overview, choosing and switching Mage The Mage class Archer The Archer class Rogue The Rogue class Warrior The Warrior class Herald The Herald class Skill trees Class perk trees, points, keystones, respec Abilities Ability keys and slots, mana, cooldowns Stats Every stat, what it does and how it trains Attributes Your Personal Attribute Prestige Prestiging a stat at its cap Gear and the forge Page What it covers Gear Slots, item levels, how gear drops Rarity The rarity ladder Weapons Weapon types and which class uses them Armour Armour pieces Sets Gear sets and their bonuses Legendary weapons The legendary weapons Relics Relics Orbs Orbs and the crafting table Gems Skill gems The forge Blacksmithing: forging, patterns, levels, mastery Ingredients Forge ingredients and modifiers, and where they drop Amulets Amulets"}, {"t": "First steps", "u": "first-steps.html", "c": "Getting started", "s": "Your first hour in Exile RPG, step by step - from the journal to your class at level 5.", "h": "1. Open the Exile's Journal 2. Talk to Warden Aldric 3. Fight, and let your stats train 4. Spend your points 5. Pick up coins 6. Watch for coloured monsters 7. Choose a class at level 5 8. Keep going Tips as you go", "x": "New to the camp? Follow these steps in order. None of them is a trap: you can't lock yourself out of anything by doing them in a different order. 1. Open the Exile's Journal Every new character gets an Exile's Journal (a glowing book). Right-click it to open your character sheet in chat. Almost everything in Exile RPG starts from here: your stats, quests, party, class, perk tree and help pages. Lost it? Reach the sheet another way (the Quest Board's [📜 Quest Log] , then [← Back] ) and click [My journal] for a new one. See The Exile's Journal . 2. Talk to Warden Aldric Warden Aldric keeps the Exile camp. Right-click him to see his menu. He gives you the story quests , one at a time: click [✔ Accept] , do the job, then come back and click [★ Turn in] . His first job is simple - go out past the fires and kill some monsters. While you're at the camp, meet the others: Who What they do Warden Aldric Story quests, his daily Bounty Board (after his second job), attribute respec The Quest Board Side quests and the daily and weekly bounties Brenna Ironhand Sells forge materials, buys your gear, counts as an anvil for the forge Hilde the Stablehand Mounts , once you've earned her trust You need to stand within about 8 blocks of a person for their menu buttons to work. 3. Fight, and let your stats train You don't pick stats from a list - they level by doing . Hitting things trains Might, taking hits trains Vitality, sprinting trains Swiftness, mining trains Mining, and so on. A popup above your hotbar shows which stat just gained XP. The full list is on the Stats page. Every stat level-up also gives you character XP , and so does most of the training XP you earn. See Leveling . 4. Spend your points Each character level gives you: 1 attribute point - spend it on the sheet with [+1] or [+5] next to a stat. Each point adds one level to that stat. 1 perk point - for your class's Perk Tree . The points wait for you until you pick a class. A title on screen and a chat line with [Stats] and [Passive Tree] buttons tell you when you level up. 5. Pick up coins Monsters drop Gold Coins , and Magic and better monsters drop more. Coins pay for almost everything: materials, mounts, respecs and class changes. See Coins and the bank . 6. Watch for coloured monsters Most monsters are plain, but some are Magic , Rare or even Champions . They're tougher, carry extra modifiers (Hasted, Armored, Vampiric, Explosive and more), and drop better loot. The level over a monster's head is green if it's 3 or more levels below you, white if it's close, and red if it's 5 or more levels above you. Monsters are levelled to the players near them when they appear, so the world grows with you. 7. Choose a class at level 5 At character level 5 a [★ Choose your class] button appears on your sheet. Pick Mage, Archer, Rogue, Warrior or Herald. Your first class is free. See Classes . 8. Keep going Take side quests from the Quest Board and claim them in your quest log - see Quests . Equip better gear as it drops. Team up with friends in a party : kills and quest progress are shared. At level 10 you can work toward your first mount . At level 20 your class opens its subclasses . Tips as you go The first time you meet something new (your first orb, your first dungeon key, your first party), a short tip appears in chat. Each tip shows once. To read them all again, click [? Help] on the character sheet. You can turn tips off or back on from that page."}, {"t": "The Exile's Journal", "u": "the-journal.html", "c": "Getting started", "s": "The Exile's Journal opens your character sheet - what every line and button on it does.", "h": "The character sheet, top to bottom Header One row per stat The lines below the stats The footer buttons Other menus you'll meet Respec of attribute points", "x": "The Exile's Journal is the glowing book every new character starts with. Right-click it and your character sheet opens in chat. Every menu in Exile RPG is a set of clickable chat buttons, and they all start here. Lost it? Click [My journal] on the sheet and you get a new one. Nothing happens if you're already carrying one. Near an anvil , right-clicking the journal opens the forge instead. Sneak while you right-click to open the sheet. Most buttons show a hover text first. Hover before you click when you're unsure. The character sheet, top to bottom Header Character Level with an XP bar and your XP toward the next level. See Leveling . A points line: your unspent attribute points , perk points and tokens . Each part is clickable and opens its own page. Your class button. Before level 5 it reads Class at Lv 5 . At level 5 it becomes [★ Choose your class] , and after that it shows your class and opens the class page. See Classes . One row per stat Each stat has its own row: You see Meaning The stat's name Click it to open the stat's own page (what it does, how it trains, its passives) Lv and a small bar The stat's level and progress to its next level [+1] / [+5] Spend 1 or 5 attribute points on this stat Aqua +N Bonus levels from gear, amulets, set bonuses and your tree ★ A new stat passive is ready to choose - click it MAX The stat is at its cap [✦] Prestige this stat - see Prestige ✦N How many times you've prestiged this stat All the details are on Stats . The lines below the stats These appear once they apply to you: Line What it is Wings Your wings and their tier, once you've earned them Marks Story marks your choices have given you (hover for what they mean) 📖 Codex How many places, secrets and voices you've found. [Open the book] puts the Codex in your bag 🐾 Bestiary How many kinds of monster you've killed. [🐾 Bestiary] shows where each comes from, what it drops and its story. More of the story opens at 10, 100 and 1000 kills 🏆 Achievements Goals done, and the title you're wearing. [🏆 Achievements] and [👑 Titles] - see Titles ❔ [? Help] Every tutorial tip in one list. Click one to read it, or turn tips off and on Encounter Shown while you're taking part in an encounter Attribute Your Personal Attribute. Hover for its card, click for its page. See Attributes 📖 [My journal] A new journal if you lost yours The footer buttons Button Opens [✚ Bonuses] Every bonus you have and where it comes from [📜 Quests] Your quest log - see Quests [👥 Party] The Party tab - see Parties [✦ Perk Tree] Your class's perk tree - see Skill trees [↺ Respec] Refund every attribute point you've spent, for coins (see below) [↻] Redraw the sheet Other menus you'll meet Quest log - also opened from the Quest Board's [📜 Quest Log] button. It has an [✦ Encounters] page listing the encounter you're in and the ones you've finished. Your Stable - sneak + right-click your Rider's Whistle. See Mounts . NPC menus - right-click Warden Aldric, Brenna Ironhand, Hilde the Stablehand or the Quest Board. Stand within about 8 blocks, or their buttons won't work. Respec of attribute points [↺ Respec] on the sheet, or [↺ Respec attributes] at Warden Aldric, gives back every attribute point you've spent so you can spend them again. You confirm with a second click. Your trained levels and stat passive choices stay as they are. The cost is 25 Gold Coins per character level (so 250 coins at level 10). Resetting your perk tree costs the same - see Skill trees ."}, {"t": "Leveling", "u": "leveling.html", "c": "Getting started", "s": "How your character level works - where character XP comes from, how much each level needs, and what levels give you.", "h": "Where character XP comes from How much XP each level needs What every level gives Milestones Your level and the world", "x": "Exile RPG has two kinds of level: Stat levels - each of your stats (Might, Vitality, Mining...) levels on its own by being used. Stats cap at 50. That's all on the Stats page. Your character level - one number for your whole character, shown at the top of the character sheet . This page is about that one. Where character XP comes from You never earn character XP directly from killing a monster. It comes from your stats and from the work you do: Source What you get A stat levels up Character XP equal to the stat's new level. Mining going from 14 to 15 gives 15 character XP Training XP A big share (half, counted before the training slow-down) of the training XP your non-gathering stats earn also counts as character XP. Gathering stats (Mining, Logging, Farming, Fishing, Cooking) and Blacksmithing give none of this share A stat at its cap (50) It keeps paying a smaller share (10%) of what it would have earned, so maxed stats still help you level Quests Many quests pay character XP directly, and story and world quests pay a lot. See Quests Bounties The Quest Board's daily and weekly bounties pay a bonus of character XP on top Dungeons and world events Finishing them pays character XP Party kills Kill XP your party shares with you trains your stats, and that counts too. See Parties Your character level is checked every few seconds, so XP from any source levels you up. If one big reward covers several levels, you climb them all at once. Prestige makes a stat train faster, but it doesn't increase the character XP share. See Prestige . How much XP each level needs The XP for your next level is 125 + 25 × your current level . It grows steadily: From level XP to the next level Total XP to reach that level 1 150 - 5 250 750 10 375 2,250 15 500 4,375 20 625 7,125 30 875 14,500 40 1,125 24,375 50 1,375 36,750 There's no hard cap on character level. What every level gives Each character level gives you: +1 attribute point - spend it on the sheet with [+1] / [+5] . Each point adds a level to a stat, up to its cap. +1 perk point - spend it on your class's Perk Tree ( Skill trees ). Points earned before you have a class wait for you. A title on screen, a sound and a chat line with [Stats] and [Passive Tree] buttons mark the moment. Milestones Level What opens 5 Choose your class (the first one is free) - Classes 10 The quest that leads to your first mount - Mounts 20 Your class's subclasses - Classes ; the title The Seasoned 40 The title The Veteran - Titles Many quests on the Quest Board also need a minimum character level before they show up. Your level and the world Monsters scale to you. A monster's level is set when it appears, from the players nearby (a little below to a few above their level). Some places have a minimum monster level. A level shown in red over a monster's head means it's 5 or more levels above you. Gear has a level. A piece only works for you while its level is within range of yours. See Gear . Bounty coins grow with you. Warden Aldric's bounties pay 2% more coins for every character level. Respecs cost more as you grow. An attribute respec or a perk tree reset costs 25 Gold Coins per character level."}, {"t": "Coins and the bank", "u": "coins-and-bank.html", "c": "Getting started", "s": "Gold Coins and Gold Bars - how you earn them, what they buy, how to sell, and keeping them safe in the Collector's bank.", "h": "Gold Coins and Gold Bars Earning coins Coins from monsters Spending coins Selling The Collector and his bank The bank Changing money", "x": "Gold Coins and Gold Bars The camp's money is the Gold Coin . A coin stack holds at most 99, so there's a bigger piece too: Item Worth Stacks to Gold Coin 1 coin 99 Gold Bar 100 coins 99 (9,900 coins in one slot) Your purse is every coin and bar you carry. Shops count both, so you never have to break a bar yourself: when you pay, loose coins go first, then a bar is broken and the change comes back as coins. The Collector (below) changes coins into bars and back. Earning coins Source Notes Monsters See the table below Quests Most quests pay coins - Quests Bounties Warden Aldric's bounties pay coins (more at higher character levels); the Quest Board's bounties add a coin bonus Dungeons and world events Finishing them pays coins Selling Brenna Ironhand and the Collector buy gear and much more (below) Coins from monsters Monster Coins Normal 35% chance of 1-2 Magic 2-5 Rare 5-10 Champion 15-30 Bosses Much more Your Fortune stat adds 3% more coins per Fortune level (bonus levels count too). Coins from a kill drop on the ground where the monster died. Spending coins What Cost Forge materials from Brenna Ironhand Her daily stock, priced per item Changing your class (the first is free) 500 Changing your subclass (the first is free) 300 Attribute respec (sheet or Warden Aldric) 25 per character level Perk tree reset 25 per character level Mounts, the Rider's Whistle and mount upgrades See Mounts If your purse is short, the bank pays the rest. Buying something you can't cover with what you carry takes the difference from your bank, and a chat line tells you how much came from each. Selling Brenna Ironhand and the Collector use one price table, so an item sells for the same at either. Prices depend on the item: gear by its type and rarity, ingredients and gems by tier, and so on. Brenna: hold the item and click [💰 Sell what you hold] , or [♻ Sell all gear] to sell every piece of gear in your bag (you pick what to keep first). The Collector: [💰 Sell what you hold] names a price, then [Deal] sells it. It only goes through for the exact item he priced. [🗑 Sell all junk] sells every junk item in your bags at once. Junk items are things some monsters drop that are only worth coins (\"Junk - The Collector buys it\" in the tooltip). Only the Collector buys junk. He also buys many vanilla items. He won't buy coins or bars, quest and story items, dungeon keys, the journal, the Rider's Whistle, or his own receipt. The Collector and his bank The Collector is a very strange trader. You meet him through the Quest Board quest The Man Who Buys Everything (from character level 4, after Warden Aldric's second job). Claiming it gives you A Receipt for Everything . Right-click the receipt and your Collector appears in front of you. He stays for 3 minutes , then can be called again 10 minutes after you summoned him. Right-clicking the receipt while he's out just reopens his menu. He leaves early if you click [👋 Dismiss] , walk 64 blocks away or log off. He's yours: only you can use your Collector. His menu shows your purse, your bank and how long he'll stay, with [💰 Sell what you hold] , [🗑 Sell all junk] , [🏦 Bank] , [⚖ Change] and [👋 Dismiss] . The bank [🏦 Bank] opens The Collector's Bank : Button Does [⬇ Deposit all] Everything in your purse goes in [⬇ 100] Deposit 100 coins [⬆ 100] / [⬆ 1000] Withdraw 100 or 1,000 coins [⬆ All] Withdraw everything The bank belongs to your character. Banked coins can't be dropped, lost or stolen - not even when you die. You only need the Collector to put coins in or take them out. Shops (Brenna, Hilde and the rest) draw on your bank automatically when your purse is short, and their menus show your bank balance. Changing money [⚖ Change] opens the money changer: [→ Coins to Bars] turns every 100 loose coins into a Gold Bar, and [→ Bars to Coins] turns bars back into coins."}, {"t": "Quests", "u": "quests.html", "c": "Getting started", "s": "How quests work - Warden Aldric's story, the Quest Board, both kinds of bounty, the quest log, tracking and claiming.", "h": "The story: Warden Aldric The Quest Board Quest Board bounties Warden Aldric's Bounty Board The quest log What quests ask of you Rewards Playing in a party", "x": "Quests come from four places: Where What Where you finish it Warden Aldric The story , one quest at a time Turn it in to Aldric The Quest Board Side quests , plus daily and weekly bounties Claim it in your quest log Aldric's Bounty Board Three quick bounties a day Turn them in to Aldric People and things in the world World quests Claim them in your quest log The story: Warden Aldric Right-click Warden Aldric at the Exile camp. His menu shows your current story quest: [✔ Accept] takes it. Hover the button first to see every stage and the reward. While you're working on it, his menu shows your progress, for example Slay 10 hostile monsters (4/10) . When it's done, come back and click [★ Turn in] for the reward. He offers story quests one at a time, in order . Some need a minimum character level, and they never count against your quest limit. When you've done everything he has for now, he says so and points you to the board. The Quest Board The Quest Board at the camp lists every side quest you can take right now : your level is high enough, you've done anything it needs first, and you're not already carrying it. Quests you've finished only come back if they're repeatable (marked ↻ ). Tabs along the top: [All] [Story] [Combat] [Gathering] [Crafting] . The board and your quest log share the tab you picked. Six quests to a page. Use [← Prev] / [Next →] . Hover a quest to see its stages, what it needs and its reward. Click [✔ Accept] to take it. [📜 Quest Log] jumps to your log, and [↻] re-reads the board. There's no claim button on the board. Finished side quests are claimed in your quest log . Quest Board bounties At the top of the board you'll see bounties picked just for you from the work you can take: How many Resets Bonus when claimed Today's bounties 3 every day of server time +25 coins, +150 character XP This week 1 every week of server time +120 coins, +600 character XP, and a 35% chance of a dungeon key Click [Take] to accept one like any other quest. Claim it in your log while it's still listed and the bonus is paid on top of the quest's normal reward, once. The board shows carrying it or bonus paid next to each one. \"Days\" here count the server's running time, not the in-game sun. Warden Aldric's Bounty Board Once you've finished Aldric's second story quest, his menu gains a [📜 Bounty Board] button with three new bounties every in-game day : Kind Example Slay a kind of monster Slay 15 Skeletons Slay Magic or Rare monsters Slay 3 Magic or Rare monsters Bring items Bring 16 Bones Slay a mini-boss Slay 1 mini-boss [Accept] a bounty, then [Abandon] it if you change your mind (its progress is lost). Kill bounties count as you go. Bring-item bounties check your bags when you turn them in. Turn finished ones in to Aldric. They pay coins (2% more per character level), XP , and the harder ones can also pay gear . The quest log Open it with [📜 Quests] on the character sheet . It lists every quest you're carrying, the stage it's on and its count: Button Does [★ Claim] Shown when a side or world quest is done. Nothing pays out by itself - click it to get the reward [Track] Puts an arrow and the distance to the quest's goal above your hotbar [Hide tracker] Clears the tracker [✖] Drops a quest [↻ Refresh] Re-checks every quest's progress and redraws the log [✦ Encounters] The encounter you're in, and the ones you've finished Finished story quests show ready: turn it in to Warden Aldric instead of a claim button. You can carry up to 85 side quests at once. Story quests don't count. The All tab groups them under Story, Combat, Gathering and Crafting headings. A ✔ Finished section at the bottom lists everything you've completed. Hover any quest name for its goals and story. What quests ask of you Each quest has one or more stages , done in order. Common kinds: Slay monsters - any monster, a kind, Magic or better, sometimes only inside a certain area. Collect items - have them in your bags. Some quests take them, and they're taken the moment the stage is complete , even right when you accept. A message tells you when that happens. Bring items to a person, or talk to someone. Reach a place, or find several spots in any order. Earn XP in a gathering stat (counted from when you accepted), reach a character level, craft or forge an item. Clear a dungeon at a tier or higher, or take part in an encounter. Some stages have conditions, like only at night , and pause while the condition isn't met. Others have a rule (for example don't die ). Breaking it starts that stage over, and a message tells you why. Rewards Quests can pay any mix of Gold Coins , character XP , stat XP , attribute or perk points , rolled gear (sized to your level), named items, dungeon keys , forge ingredients and modifiers , Blacksmithing XP , and even a mount . The reward is shown on the offer's hover. Repeatable quests pay less, and some of their rewards are random. Playing in a party Party members share quest progress: kills, places reached, spots found, people talked to and encounters finished all count for every member carrying the same quest. Items you hand in or collect are your own. See Parties ."}, {"t": "Parties", "u": "parties.html", "c": "Getting started", "s": "Making a party, inviting and managing members, the Party tab, and what a party shares - kill XP, quest progress and dungeon runs.", "h": "Opening the Party tab Running a party The Party tab What a party shares No friendly fire Party chat Kill XP Quest progress Dungeons Common questions", "x": "A party is a group you make on purpose. It has no size limit and lasts across logouts : you stay in it until you leave, are kicked, or it's disbanded. Opening the Party tab Click [👥 Party] in the footer of the character sheet . Not in a party yet? Click [+ Create party] to start one with you as leader, or [⚙ Invite people] . Inviting someone also creates the party. In a party, the tab shows one member at a time (see below). Running a party Action Who How Invite The leader [⚙ Manage] lists everyone online who isn't in your party, nearest first, 10 to a page ( [More ▶] ). Click [+ Invite] Accept / Decline The invited player [Accept] or [Decline] on the invite. It lasts 60 seconds . Accepting while you're in another party moves you over Leave Anyone [✖ Leave] , then [Yes, leave] Kick The leader [⚙ Manage] > [✖ Kick] , then [Yes, kick] . Works on offline members too, and they're told when they next log in Make leader The leader [⚙ Manage] > [★ Make leader] (online members only) Disband The leader [⚙ Manage] > [Disband] , then [Yes, disband] A player can hold only one invite at a time. A newer one replaces it. If the leader leaves, the member who joined earliest takes over (an online one first). A leader who hasn't been online for a full in-game day loses the lead to the earliest online member. When the last member leaves, the party ends. Members see [⚙ Members] instead of [⚙ Manage] : the member list and [✖ Leave] . The Party tab The tab fits one member on one chat screen, with a strip of every member at the top. Click a name in the strip, or use [◀ Prev] / [Next ▶] , to switch. You're in the strip too, so you can see what your party sees of you. Left: everything they're wearing and holding - main hand, off hand, armour and the three amulet slots. Hover an item for its full tooltip. Middle: level and XP bar, class and subclass, ultimate, health, mana, unspent points and tokens, prestiges, where they are and the day they joined. Right: their stats with short names (VIT, MIG, FER...), each with its level, gear bonus and prestige stars. Bottom lines: set pieces and set ultimate, mount, titles and wings. Hover them for details. In the strip, ★ marks the leader and ○ an offline member. An offline member's page shows what the server last knew about them and when they were last seen. [↻] reads their gear again. What a party shares No friendly fire Party members can't hurt each other with melee or arrows, and class spells only ever hit monsters. Members also see each other faintly while invisible. Players outside any party can't hurt each other either - see Death and safety . Party chat The party is a Minecraft team, so Minecraft's team chat ( /teammsg , or /tm ) talks only to your party. Kill XP A kill trains 5 Ferocity XP for the killer. In a party, every other member within 200 blocks gets that XP too: Where the member is They get The killer keeps Within 30 blocks of the kill The full kill XP All of it 30-200 blocks away 25% 75% (when at least one member is that far out) On top of that, a big level gap cuts a member's share: Level difference from the killer Share 0-5 100% 6-10 50% 11-20 20% 21+ 5% Small shares add up over many kills. Shared XP trains Ferocity like any other XP, so it also gives character XP . Quest progress Party members carrying the same quest share its progress, wherever they are : kills, zones and places reached, spots found, people talked to and encounters finished. Item hand-ins and collecting stay your own. Loot, coins and keys from a kill stay with the killer. Members on the death screen don't receive kill XP or quest kills. Dungeons In a party, only the leader starts a dungeon run (using their key). Members near the entrance come in with them. Members who are too far away, or in another world, get a [Join] button for 60 seconds that teleports them into the run. A key used by a non-leader is refused with a message. Common questions Does my party survive a logout or a server restart? Yes. Is there a size cap? No. Can I see a member's gear while they're offline? Yes, as it was when the server last saw it."}, {"t": "Mounts", "u": "mounts.html", "c": "Getting started", "s": "Getting your first mount from Hilde the Stablehand, using the Rider's Whistle, your stable, and mount upgrades.", "h": "Your first mount: Hilde the Stablehand What Hilde sells The Rider's Whistle Your Stable Upgrades Ironhide's Slash", "x": "Mounts carry you across the world much faster than your feet. You own mounts and keep them forever, and you call whichever one you've equipped with a single item: the Rider's Whistle . Your first mount: Hilde the Stablehand Hilde the Stablehand runs the stable. She doesn't sell mounts to strangers. First you have to finish the Quest Board quest Hilde's First Mount : It appears on the board from character level 10 , once you're far enough into Warden Aldric's story. She asks for 24 Wheat (feed) and 6 Leather (tack), then a hand at the stable (talk to her). It pays 150 coins and some character XP, and from then on Hilde will sell to you. Until then her menu reminds you what she's waiting for. The Rider's Whistle is always for sale. What Hilde sells Mount Price What it's like Attack Dustmane 600 coins A steady camp horse: quick and reliable None Ironhide 2,500 coins A war bear: slower than a horse, with twice the health Slash Rider's Whistle 500 coins A spare whistle - Your first mount comes with a whistle for free . Her menu shows your purse and bank together, and a purchase draws on your bank if your purse is short. The rarer mounts aren't hers to sell: they're won elsewhere in the world. The Rider's Whistle One whistle calls every mount you own . It works from your main hand or your off hand. What you do What happens Right-click on foot Calls your equipped mount and puts you in the saddle Sneak + right-click on foot Opens Your Stable Right-click in the saddle Your mount's attack , if it has one Sneak in the saddle Get off. The mount fades about 3 seconds later Only you can ride your called mount. Anyone else who climbs on is put straight back down. A mount nobody is riding fades after a few seconds. You can always whistle it back. Mounts can be hurt and killed. A fallen mount isn't lost - just call it again. Mounts can't follow you into a dungeon . Entering one sets you down. Your spells and abilities never hit mounts. Your Stable Sneak + right-click the whistle to open Your Stable . It lists every mount you own: [Equip] picks which mount the whistle calls. speed and attack show each mount's upgrade levels (0-5), each with a [+15% · cost] button to buy the next level. Upgrades Each mount upgrades separately . Every level adds +15% , so five levels is +75%: Level 1 2 3 4 5 Cost (Gold Coins) 500 1,200 2,500 5,000 10,000 Speed - every mount. Takes effect immediately, even on the mount you're sitting on. Attack - only mounts with an attack. Raises that attack's damage. Ironhide's Slash Ironhide swipes a heavy blow through everything just ahead of it. It has a 3-second cooldown and hits every monster in front of the bear."}, {"t": "Titles", "u": "titles.html", "c": "Getting started", "s": "Achievements, the titles they give, and how to wear a title above your head.", "h": "Achievements Some achievements to aim for Dungeon records Wearing a title", "x": "A title is a short phrase you can wear, shown above your head for everyone to see. You earn titles from achievements , from some deeds and encounters out in the world, and from setting dungeon records. Achievements Click [🏆 Achievements] on the character sheet . The page lists every goal in the game: ✔ - earned, ○ - not yet. Each line says what the goal takes and, if it gives one, its title . Achievements are checked every 20 seconds. When you earn one, chat announces it with a sound, and its title is yours to wear. Earned achievements stay earned. The sheet's achievements line shows how many you've earned and the title you're wearing. Some achievements to aim for Achievement Goal Title First Steps Reach character level 5 - Seasoned Reach character level 20 The Seasoned Veteran Reach character level 40 The Veteran Hand for Hire Finish 10 quests - Board Regular Finish 30 quests The Reliable Tireless Finish 60 quests The Tireless Gravedigger Put down 100 of the shambling dead Gravedigger Thousandfold A thousand of the shambling dead The Thousandfold There are many more: story milestones, dungeons and their tiers, every secret of a region, monster hunting and more. The Achievements page has the full list. Dungeon records Each dungeon keeps a record time per tier . Beat it and you hold the title Fleetfoot of that dungeon. Records can be beaten, and when someone beats yours the title passes to them and is taken off you automatically. Wearing a title Click [👑 Titles] , on the character sheet or on the Achievements page: Wearing: shows your current title. Every title you hold is listed with [Wear] . [Take the title off] wears none. You wear one title at a time . It floats on a line above your head , whether or not you're in a party."}, {"t": "Death and safety", "u": "death-and-safety.html", "c": "Getting started", "s": "What happens when you die, what you always keep, keeping coins safe, dungeon lives, protected areas and the no-PvP rule.", "h": "No PvP When you die Dying in a dungeon Protected areas Staying safe out there", "x": "No PvP Exile RPG is a cooperative game. Players can't hurt each other: Everyone who isn't in a party is on the same peaceful team, with friendly fire off. Each party is its own team, also with friendly fire off, so party members can't hurt each other either. Class spells and abilities only ever hit monsters, never players, animals, villagers or mounts. When you die Your character never loses progress when you die. You always keep: Kept Character level and XP Nothing is taken away Every stat level and its XP, prestiges, stat passives Your class, subclass, perk tree and attribute Your quests and their progress Except stages with a \"don't die\" rule (below) Coins in the bank Banked coins can't be dropped or lost Your mounts, titles, achievements, codex and bestiary Your party What you're carrying follows the world's normal Minecraft rules: if the world drops items on death, your gear, coins and bars drop where you died like any other items. That's the best reason to bank coins with the Collector. See Coins and the bank . A few things do end when you die: An active set ultimate ends, and some short-lived class effects reset. Charges your attribute was building up are lost. Your mount fades once nobody is riding it. Whistle it back after you respawn. A quest stage with a \"don't die\" rule, or one that asks you to survive a stretch of time, starts over. The quest tells you when it does. When you respawn, all your stat and gear bonuses come back on their own. Dying in a dungeon Dungeons have their own rules: You have 3 lives per run . Each death costs one, and the party is told how many you have left. After a death you wait for the party . You rejoin when they reach the next checkpoint or the boss. If the whole party falls , the dungeon brings everyone back to the last checkpoint. There are no revives. With no lives left you're out of that run: you can watch the party, or leave. Every respawn message has a [Leave the dungeon] button. Protected areas Some places in the world, like camps and story sites, are protected . While you're inside one you're in adventure mode: you can't break or place blocks. A short line above your hotbar tells you when you walk in, and you're back to normal the moment you leave. Dungeons work the same way. Staying safe out there Read the level over a monster's head. Green is 3 or more levels below you, white is close, and red is 5 or more levels above you . Respect coloured monsters. Magic, Rare and Champion monsters carry modifiers. An Explosive one goes off when it dies, so step back. Bank your coins before a risky trip. Bring friends. A party shares kill XP and quest progress, and can't hurt itself."}, {"t": "FAQ", "u": "faq.html", "c": "Getting started", "s": "Quick answers to the questions new exiles ask most.", "h": "The basics Leveling and stats Quests Coins Gear Friends and parties Mounts Dying", "x": "The basics How do I open the menus? Right-click the Exile's Journal . Every menu is a set of clickable buttons in chat. See The Exile's Journal . I lost my journal. Get back to the character sheet another way and click [My journal] for a new one. Right-click the Quest Board, click [📜 Quest Log] , then [← Back] for the sheet. The [Stats] button in a level-up message works too. The journal opened the forge instead of my sheet. Right-clicking it next to an anvil opens the forge . Sneak while you right-click to get the sheet. An NPC's buttons do nothing. Stand closer. You need to be within about 8 blocks of Warden Aldric, Brenna Ironhand or Hilde the Stablehand for their buttons to work. Can I turn the chat tips off? Yes. Click [? Help] on the character sheet, then [Turn tips off] . You can read every tip there at any time. Leveling and stats How do I level up? Use your stats. Fighting, sprinting, blocking, mining and the rest train them, and stat training feeds your character XP. Quests and bounties pay character XP directly. See Leveling . What do I spend points on? Each character level gives one attribute point (spend it with [+1] / [+5] on the sheet) and one perk point (for your class's Perk Tree). See Stats and Skill trees . I spent my attribute points badly. Click [↺ Respec] on the sheet, or [↺ Respec attributes] at Warden Aldric. It costs 25 Gold Coins per character level and refunds every spent point. When do I get a class? At character level 5. Your first class is free, and changing later costs 500 coins. See Classes . Quests I finished a quest but got nothing. Side quests and world quests have to be claimed : open [📜 Quests] and click [★ Claim] . Story quests are turned in to Warden Aldric with [★ Turn in] . See Quests . Some of my items vanished when I took a quest. Some quests take the items they ask for the moment that stage is complete, even right as you accept. Chat tells you when that happens. How many quests can I carry? Up to 85 side quests, plus your story quest. Where do I find bounties? Two places: the Quest Board (3 a day and 1 a week, with bonus pay) and Warden Aldric's Bounty Board (3 a day, after his second story quest). How do I find where a quest wants me to go? Click [Track] next to it in the quest log. An arrow and the distance show above your hotbar. Coins How do I keep my coins safe? Put them in the Collector's bank . Banked coins can't be dropped or lost, and shops use them automatically when your purse is short. You meet the Collector through the Quest Board quest The Man Who Buys Everything . See Coins and the bank . My coins don't fit in one stack. A Gold Bar is worth 100 coins. The Collector's [⚖ Change] turns 100 coins into a bar, and shops accept bars as they are. Where do I sell gear? Brenna Ironhand ( [💰 Sell what you hold] , [♻ Sell all gear] ) or the Collector. Both pay the same prices. Gear My new weapon does no damage. Gear only works when its level is within range of yours. Check the item's level. See Gear . My helmet in my hand gives nothing. Gear only counts in its own slot. Friends and parties Can other players hurt me? No. Players outside a party can't hurt each other, and party members can't hurt each other. See Death and safety . Do we share XP in a party? Yes. Kill XP is shared with members within 200 blocks (in full within 30 blocks), and quest progress is shared wherever members are. See Parties . Who opens the dungeon in a party? Only the party leader can start a run with a key. Far-away members get a [Join] button. Mounts How do I get a mount? Finish Hilde's First Mount on the Quest Board (from level 10), then buy Dustmane (600 coins) or Ironhide (2,500) from Hilde the Stablehand. See Mounts . My mount disappeared. A mount nobody rides fades after a few seconds. Right-click the Rider's Whistle to call it back. Can I bring my mount into a dungeon? No. Entering a dungeon sets you down. Dying What do I lose when I die? No character progress at all. Your levels, stats, quests, mounts and banked coins are safe. Items you carry follow the world's normal death rules. See Death and safety ."}, {"t": "Classes", "u": "classes.html", "c": "Classes & stats", "s": "The five classes of Exile RPG, how to choose one at level 5, subclasses at level 20, and what switching costs.", "h": "Classes at a glance Choosing your class Subclasses Switching What your class changes Unarmed and off-class weapons", "x": "Classes at a glance A class gives you a weapon to cast with, a set of abilities on your keys, a perk tree of your own and gear that suits your playstyle. There are five classes. Each one splits into two or three subclasses later on. Class Role Casts with Scales with Subclasses (level 20) ✧ Mage Spirit nuker: big magic damage from range Any staff or Mage Blade Arcana, Might, Precision Pyromancer, Frostbinder ➶ Archer Long, sustained damage with high mobility Any bow or crossbow Precision, Swiftness Sniper, Windrunner ✦ Rogue Utility, debuffs and high burst damage Any dagger Swiftness, Precision, Ferocity, Fortune Assassin, Poisoner, Duelist ⚔ Warrior Versatile damage dealer or tank Any sword, axe or mace Might, Fortitude, Toughness, Vitality, Steadfast Paladin, Berserker ⚑ Herald Support: heals, buffs your party, hexes enemies Herald’s Standard Arcana, Vitality, Fortune Hymnwarden, Warchanter Choosing your class Classes unlock at character level 5 (see Leveling ). Until then you play without abilities. Once you reach level 5, your character sheet shows [★ Choose your class] . Right-click your Exile’s Journal to open the sheet. On the class page, pick a class with [Choose] and confirm with [Yes] . Your first class is free. Unless you already carry one of the class's weapons, you get a Common starter weapon: the Arcane Staff (Mage), Hunter’s Bow (Archer), Assassin’s Dagger (Rogue), Warblade (Warrior) or Herald’s Standard (Herald). Subclasses At character level 20 the class page's Subclass row unlocks. A subclass does two things: It upgrades one of your base abilities into a stronger version. For example, a Pyromancer's Mana Shield becomes Flame Shield. It gives you an ultimate. Mages, Rogues and Warriors get their ultimate on a fourth key. An Archer's ultimate replaces Piercing Shot. Heralds already have an ultimate, and a subclass swaps it for a different one. Your first subclass is free. Switching Change Cost First class free Changing class 500 gold coins First subclass free Changing subclass 300 gold coins There is no daily limit. When you change class: - your subclass is cleared, so you choose again for the new class. - every point in your old class's perk tree comes back to you for free. The new class's tree starts empty. Your Trades nodes stay (see Skill trees ). - your ability keys go back to their defaults. Changing subclass resets your keys as well. Coins are covered on Coins and bank . What your class changes Faster training for your class stats. Your class's scaling stats (the \"Scales with\" column above) earn +25% training XP . See Stats . Abilities. You cast while holding your class weapon. Keys, mana and cooldowns work the same for every class: see Abilities . A perk tree. Every class has its own tree with three build branches: see Skill trees . Gear. Class gear rolls favour that class's stats. Warriors' armour rolls the most armour and Mages' the least. See Gear and Armour . Amulets. Some amulets have a passive that only works for one class. See Amulets . Unarmed and off-class weapons Your stats (Might, perks, tree nodes, stat passives) only apply while you hold a real weapon: a sword, axe, mace, trident, bow, crossbow or Exile RPG weapon. With an empty hand or a tool you punch for exactly 1 heart. Abilities only cast while you hold a weapon of your own class."}, {"t": "Mage", "u": "mage.html", "c": "Classes & stats", "s": "The Mage is a spirit nuker who deals big magic damage from range with a staff or Mage Blade and scales with Arcana.", "h": "Role Weapons and armour Abilities Subclasses Perk tree Battlemage Elementalist Warden Good to know", "x": "Role The Mage is a spirit nuker . You fire bolts and beams from range, protect yourself with a mana shield, and at level 20 add fire or ice. Most of your damage comes from Arcana , which also raises your max mana. Might and Precision add to some spells. Role Ranged magic damage Casts with Any staff or Mage Blade Starter weapon Arcane Staff Armour Light: robes and soft leather looks. Mage armour rolls the least armour of any class. Scales with (+25% training XP) Arcana, Might, Precision Keys 4: Right-click, Sneak + Right-click, F, Sneak + F Subclasses (level 20) Pyromancer, Frostbinder Weapons and armour Staffs are the classic Mage weapon. A Mage Blade is a light blade that also casts your spells and hits harder in melee than a staff. Both count as your class weapon, so you can cast with either. Their affixes lean towards Arcana. Mage gear rolls mostly Arcana , then Vitality and Precision. It rarely rolls Ferocity, Fortitude, Toughness or Steadfast. The Forbidden Amulet has a passive that only works for Mages. See Amulets . See Weapons and Armour for the full lists. Abilities Damage is a base number plus a share of your stat levels. For example, \"6 + 40% Arcana\" at Arcana 30 deals 6 + 12 = 18. To see your current numbers, hover an ability on the class page. See Abilities for how keys, mana and cooldowns work. Key Ability Mana Cooldown What it does Damage Right-click Arcane Bolt 10 0.6 s A fast bolt of spirit energy (24 blocks). 6 + 40% Arcana Sneak + Right-click Spirit Lance 25 4 s A beam that pierces every enemy in a 14-block line. 8 + 50% Arcana + 30% Precision F Mana Shield 20 15 s For 6 s nothing gets through. Every hit drains 2 mana per point of damage it would have done, and the shield breaks when your mana runs out. none Sneak + F Ultimate Unlocks with your subclass. Subclasses Subclass Upgrade (F) Ultimate (Sneak + F) Pyromancer (fire) Flame Shield (20 mana, 15 s): a Mana Shield that also gives Fire Resistance. Hostile mobs that hit you catch fire and take 2 + 15% Arcana + 15% Might. Meteor (60 mana, 40 s): calls a meteor down where you look (30 blocks). It lands 1 s later and explodes in a 5-block radius for 20 + 100% Arcana + 50% Might, setting enemies on fire. Frostbinder (ice and control) Ice Barrier (20 mana, 15 s): a Mana Shield. While it holds, hostile mobs within 4 blocks get Slowness II. Blizzard (60 mana, 40 s): a 6 s ice storm where you look, radius 6. It deals 2 + 10% Arcana every half second and applies Slowness III. Perk tree Every class tree has the same shape: a shared trunk and three branches, each ending in a keystone. How points, refunds and the Trades nodes work is on Skill trees . Small nodes give bonus stat levels. They work like gear bonuses: they can go past the cap, but they don't unlock perks. Battlemage The left branch, entered from Thick Hide. Small nodes: 7 × +2 Arcana, 3 × +2 Precision. Node Type Cost Effect Rapid Bolt Notable 1 Arcane Bolt cooldown 0.4 s (was 0.6). Split Bolt Notable 1 Arcane Bolt fires two bolts at 60% each. Spellweaver Notable 1 +2 damage on every ability hit. Chain Notable 1 A kill with Arcane Bolt refunds its mana. Arcane Barrage ★ Keystone 3 Arcane Bolt fires THREE bolts at 45% each. Spirit Lance costs double mana. Elementalist The middle branch, entered from Light Feet. Small nodes: 7 × +2 Arcana, 3 × +1 Might. Node Type Cost Effect Mana Font Notable 1 +40 max mana. Quick Wit Notable 1 Ability cooldowns are 10% shorter. Overcharge Notable 1 All ability stat ratios count 25% higher. -15% max health. Long Lance Notable 1 Spirit Lance reaches 20 blocks (was 14). Overcharge Mastery ★ Keystone 3 All ability stat ratios count 50% higher. -30% max health. Arcane Bolt costs 20 mana. Warden The right branch, entered from Second Breath. Small nodes: 7 × +2 Arcana, 3 × +1 Might. Node Type Cost Effect Mana Tide Notable 1 +2 mana per second. Thick Shield Notable 1 Mana Shield (and its upgrades) last 8 s (was 6). Reflect Notable 1 Your shield returns 25% of what it stopped to the attacker. Blood Pact Notable 1 Below 20 mana your abilities cost health instead (1 per 5 mana). Blood Magic ★ Keystone 3 Abilities cost health instead of mana: 1 health per 5 mana. Mana Shield becomes a Health Shield that costs 10 health. Good to know Mana is 100 + 4 per Arcana level and refills 5% of your max every second. Arcana trains as you spend mana (and when you enchant), so casting a lot makes you stronger. See Stats . The Arcana perks and passives on the Stats page are mostly Mage tools: Mana Flow, Spell Echo, Arcane Mastery, Archmage, Deep Reserves, Arcane Surge and more. Each branch has a different focus. Battlemage builds around Arcane Bolt. Elementalist raises every ability's damage but costs you health. Warden builds around your shield and mana. With Blood Magic you cast with health instead of mana, so it pairs well with Vitality and healing."}, {"t": "Archer", "u": "archer.html", "c": "Classes & stats", "s": "The Archer deals long, sustained damage with any bow or crossbow, moves fast, and scales with Precision and Swiftness.", "h": "Role Weapons and armour Abilities Subclasses Perk tree Rain Marksman Skirmisher Good to know", "x": "Role The Archer deals long, sustained damage with high mobility . Your normal arrows do most of the work. Your abilities add explosions, piercing shots and an escape. Precision raises all your arrow damage, and Swiftness keeps you out of reach. Role Ranged physical damage, kiting Casts with Any bow or crossbow (vanilla ones too) Starter weapon Hunter’s Bow (if you don't already carry a bow) Armour Light leather looks. Archer armour rolls less armour than a Warrior's. Scales with (+25% training XP) Precision, Swiftness Keys 3: Sneak + draw, F, Sneak + F Subclasses (level 20) Sniper, Windrunner Weapons and armour Any bow or crossbow is your class weapon, from a plain vanilla bow to a rolled Exile RPG bow. Archer gear rolls mostly Precision and Swiftness . It rarely rolls Might, Fortitude, Toughness or Arcana. The Eagle Eye amulet has a passive that only works for Archers. See Amulets . Cheap arrows: 1 cobblestone on top of 2 wood planks (any planks, in one column of the crafting grid) makes 4 arrows . The recipe appears in an Archer's recipe book. See Weapons and Armour for the full lists. Abilities You have three keys . To cast your first ability, start drawing your bow while sneaking. Damage is a base number plus a share of your stat levels: \"7 + 50% Precision\" at Precision 30 deals 7 + 15 = 22. Hover an ability on the class page to see your numbers. See Abilities for how keys, mana and cooldowns work. Key Ability Mana Cooldown What it does Damage Sneak + draw Blast Arrow 15 5 s Fires one spirit arrow (30 blocks) that explodes where it lands. Everything within 3 blocks takes the damage. 7 + 50% Precision F Disengage 15 8 s Spin up into the air, then drift down with Slow Falling. Hostile mobs within 4 blocks get Slowness II for 3 s. none Sneak + F Piercing Shot 25 10 s An instant shot that pierces every enemy in a 30-block line. 8 + 60% Precision + 20% Swiftness Subclasses An Archer's subclass ultimate replaces Piercing Shot on Sneak + F. Subclass Upgrade Ultimate (replaces Piercing Shot) Sniper (long range) Shredding Shot replaces Blast Arrow (15 mana, 5 s): a shredding arrow through everything in a 30-block line for 8 + 55% Precision. Shredded enemies take 20% more damage for 5 s and bleed for 4 s. Deadshot (50 mana, 30 s): a devastating shot through everything in a 50-block line for 20 + 120% Precision. Slows what it hits. Windrunner (mobility) Tailwind replaces Disengage (15 mana, 7 s): dash forward on the wind and gain Speed II for 4 s. Gale Barrage (50 mana, 30 s): for 2 s you fire a spirit arrow every quarter second where you look, each for 2 + 15% Precision + 15% Swiftness, with Speed II. Perk tree Every class tree has the same shape: a shared trunk and three branches, each ending in a keystone. How points, refunds and the Trades nodes work is on Skill trees . Small nodes give bonus stat levels. They work like gear bonuses: they can go past the cap, but they don't unlock perks. \"Normal arrows\" means the arrows you shoot with your bow, not ability arrows. Rain The left branch, entered from Thick Hide. Small nodes: 7 × +2 Precision, 3 × +2 Swiftness. Node Type Cost Effect Barbed Arrows Notable 1 Normal arrows make what they hit bleed for 3 s (1 + 5% Precision a second). Concussive Blast Notable 1 Blast Arrow slows what it hits (Slowness II, 3 s). Shatter Notable 1 Monsters caught in Blast Arrow take 15% more damage from everyone for 4 s. Arrow Storm Notable 1 Gale Barrage and Deadshot last / reach 25% more. Rain of Ruin ★ Keystone 3 Every normal arrow hit stacks Exposed on the monster: +5% damage taken from everyone per stack, up to 3 stacks (15%), for 5 s. Your normal arrows deal 20% less. Marksman The middle branch, entered from Light Feet. Small nodes: 7 × +2 Precision, 3 × +2 Swiftness. Node Type Cost Effect Heavy Draw Notable 1 Arrows +20% damage; your bow draws 15% slower. Headshot Notable 1 Arrow hits from 15+ blocks are criticals (+50%). Steady Hands Notable 1 +20% arrow damage while sneaking. Long Shot Notable 1 Piercing Shot reaches 45 blocks. Deadeye Focus ★ Keystone 3 Piercing Shot deals double damage and slows what it hits. Blast Arrow has half the radius. Skirmisher The right branch, entered from Second Breath. Small nodes: 7 × +2 Swiftness, 3 × +2 Precision. Node Type Cost Effect Fleet Archer Notable 1 Speed I while holding a bow or crossbow. Kiting Notable 1 Arrows shot while you are moving deal +15%. Spring Heels Notable 1 Disengage: 2 s shorter cooldown and 2 blocks higher. Second Wind Notable 1 Disengage gives you 2 hearts of Absorption. Parting Shot ★ Keystone 3 After Disengage your next arrow within 5 s is charged: it explodes on impact for 8 + 60% Precision to hostile mobs within 3 blocks. Disengage costs double mana. Good to know Each Precision level gives +2% damage on arrows you shoot. It trains every time your arrows hit a mob. See Stats . The Precision perks and passives (Steady Aim, Swift Shot, Hamstring, Deadeye, Eagle Eye, Sharpshooter and more) are Archer staples. \"Exposed\" from Shatter, Rain of Ruin and Shredding Shot makes a monster take more damage from everyone , so it helps your whole party . When more than one of them applies, only the strongest counts. It is half as strong on bosses."}, {"t": "Rogue", "u": "rogue.html", "c": "Classes & stats", "s": "The Rogue is a dagger fighter who teleports behind enemies, blinds and poisons them, and hits hard, scaling with Swiftness, Precision, Ferocity and Fortune.", "h": "Role Weapons and armour Abilities Subclasses Perk tree Assassin Blade Dancer Shadow Good to know", "x": "Role The Rogue brings utility, debuffs and high burst damage . You step behind your target, throw knives, vanish in smoke and finish enemies off with fast dagger hits. Of all the classes, the Rogue spreads its power over the most stats: Swiftness, Precision, Ferocity and Fortune . Role Melee burst, debuffs, evasion Casts with Any dagger Starter weapon Assassin’s Dagger Armour Light leather looks. Rogue armour rolls less armour than a Warrior's. Scales with (+25% training XP) Swiftness, Precision, Ferocity, Fortune Keys 4: Right-click, Sneak + Right-click, F, Sneak + F Subclasses (level 20) Assassin, Poisoner, Duelist Weapons and armour Every dagger is a Rogue class weapon. Daggers attack fast and cast your abilities. Rogue gear rolls mostly Ferocity , Precision , Swiftness and Fortune . It rarely rolls Might, Fortitude, Toughness, Vitality or Arcana. The Thief’s Gold amulet has a passive that only works for Rogues. See Amulets . See Weapons and Armour for the full lists. Abilities Damage is a base number plus a share of your stat levels: \"4 + 40% Ferocity + 20% Swiftness\" at Ferocity 30 and Swiftness 20 deals 4 + 12 + 4 = 20. Hover an ability on the class page to see your numbers. See Abilities for how keys, mana and cooldowns work. Key Ability Mana Cooldown What it does Damage Right-click Shadowstep 15 5 s Teleport behind the enemy you look at (10 blocks). Your next melee hit within 3 s deals bonus damage. bonus 4 + 40% Ferocity + 20% Swiftness Sneak + Right-click Fan of Knives 20 6 s Throw 5 knives in a cone (10 blocks). 3 + 25% Precision + 15% Ferocity per knife F Smoke Bomb 25 15 s Hostile mobs within 5 blocks are blinded and slowed for 3 s. You turn invisible and fast for 3 s. none Sneak + F Ultimate Unlocks with your subclass. Subclasses Subclass Upgrade Ultimate (Sneak + F) Assassin (burst) Ambush replaces Shadowstep (15 mana, 5 s): a Shadowstep that also turns you invisible for 2 s. Your next melee hit deals 8 + 70% Ferocity + 30% Swiftness + 20% Fortune bonus damage. Death Mark (50 mana, 40 s): mark the enemy you look at (16 blocks). After 3 s the mark bursts for 10 + 80% Ferocity + 50% Precision + 30% Fortune. Poisoner (damage over time) Venom Fan replaces Fan of Knives (20 mana, 6 s): 5 poisoned knives in a cone (10 blocks), each for 3 + 25% Precision + 15% Fortune, plus Poison II for 4 s. Plague (50 mana, 40 s): a 6 s toxic cloud where you look (radius 5). It deals 1 + 8% Precision + 8% Fortune every half second and poisons. Duelist (counters) Riposte Stance replaces Smoke Bomb (25 mana, 12 s): a 2 s stance with Resistance III. Every hostile mob that hits you is countered for 6 + 50% Ferocity + 30% Precision. Blade Dance (50 mana, 40 s): a 2 s whirl of blades that hits every enemy within 6 blocks 8 times, for 2 + 10% Ferocity + 10% Swiftness each hit. You have Resistance I and Speed II while dancing. Perk tree Every class tree has the same shape: a shared trunk and three branches, each ending in a keystone. How points, refunds and the Trades nodes work is on Skill trees . Small nodes give bonus stat levels. They work like gear bonuses: they can go past the cap, but they don't unlock perks. Assassin The left branch, entered from Thick Hide. Small nodes: 4 × +2 Ferocity, 3 × +2 Precision, 3 × +2 Swiftness. Node Type Cost Effect Keen Edge Notable 1 +1 melee damage and +0.1 attack speed. Backstab Notable 1 Shadowstep's bonus hit deals +50% when it lands from behind. Opportunist Notable 1 +3 melee damage to mobs that are slowed, blinded or poisoned. Marked Prey Notable 1 Death Mark goes off after 2 s (was 3). Cutthroat ★ Keystone 3 Melee hits on mobs below 30% health deal double damage; you take 15% more damage. Fan of Knives throws 3 knives. Blade Dancer The middle branch, entered from Light Feet. Small nodes: 4 × +2 Swiftness, 3 × +2 Ferocity, 3 × +2 Precision. Node Type Cost Effect Battle Fury Notable 1 +0.25 attack speed. Twin Knives Notable 1 Fan of Knives throws 7 knives, each -15%. Flurry Notable 1 Every 4th melee hit ignores your weapon's attack cooldown (full damage). Riposte Notable 1 Riposte Stance counters for +30%. Thousand Cuts ★ Keystone 3 Every melee hit is two hits at 60% each. Shadowstep is gone from your keys. Shadow The right branch, entered from Second Breath. Small nodes: 4 × +2 Swiftness, 3 × +2 Fortune, 3 × +2 Ferocity. Node Type Cost Effect Fleetfoot Notable 1 +8% move speed. Night Stalker Notable 1 At night: Night Vision and Speed I. Long Smoke Notable 1 Smoke Bomb lasts 5 s with a radius of 7. Evasion Notable 1 When a hostile mob hits you: 10% chance of Resistance III for 1 s. Ghost ★ Keystone 3 Smoke Bomb's invisibility lasts until you hit something; your hits from invisibility deal +40%. -2 armour. Good to know Thousand Cuts also locks out Ambush, the Assassin's version of Shadowstep. Plan your subclass around it. Some notables need a particular subclass to do anything: Marked Prey (Assassin's Death Mark) and Riposte (Duelist's Riposte Stance). Ferocity trains on kills and gives attack speed. Fortune trains from fresh loot chests, villager trades and enchanting, and adds luck. See Stats ."}, {"t": "Warrior", "u": "warrior.html", "c": "Classes & stats", "s": "The Warrior fights with any sword, axe or mace and can deal heavy melee damage or tank, scaling with Might, Fortitude, Toughness, Vitality and Steadfast.", "h": "Role Weapons, armour and the shield Abilities Subclasses Perk tree Juggernaut Berserker Crusader Good to know", "x": "Role The Warrior is a versatile damage dealer or tank . You slam the ground, leap into packs of monsters and harden your skin with Absorption. Build for raw damage with Might , or for staying power with Fortitude, Toughness, Vitality and Steadfast . A Warrior has the most scaling stats of any class. Role Melee damage or tank Casts with Any sword , axe or mace (vanilla ones too) Starter weapon Warblade (if you carry no sword, axe or mace) Armour Heavy: iron and chainmail looks. Warrior armour rolls the most armour of any class (×1.3). Scales with (+25% training XP) Might, Fortitude, Toughness, Vitality, Steadfast Keys 4: Right-click, Sneak + Right-click, F, Sneak + F Subclasses (level 20) Paladin, Berserker Weapons, armour and the shield Every sword, axe and mace is a Warrior class weapon, from vanilla iron to rolled Exile RPG gear. Warriors cannot block. Your weapon's right-click casts abilities instead. Shield stance: a shield in your offhand gives +2 armour, +1 armour toughness and -15% damage . The damage penalty applies to melee and abilities alike. Effects that say \"with a shield in your offhand\" or \"blocking\" trigger when a monster hits you while you hold a shield in your offhand . This includes Fortitude training, the Fortitude perks and passives, and the tree's Shield Master and Iron Fortress. Falls, fire and other damage don't count. Warrior gear rolls mostly Fortitude , Toughness , Vitality , Might and Steadfast . It rarely rolls Ferocity, Precision or Arcana. The Warrior’s Boon amulet has a passive that only works for Warriors. See Amulets . See Weapons and Armour for the full lists. Abilities Damage is a base number plus a share of your stat levels: \"5 + 40% Might + 20% Steadfast\" at Might 30 and Steadfast 20 deals 5 + 12 + 4 = 21. Hover an ability on the class page to see your numbers. See Abilities for how keys, mana and cooldowns work. Key Ability Mana Cooldown What it does Damage Right-click Ground Slam 20 6 s Slam the ground. Hostile mobs within 6 blocks are hit and knocked into the air. 5 + 40% Might + 20% Steadfast Sneak + Right-click Crashing Slam 15 8 s Leap forward and crash down, damaging hostile mobs within 5.5 blocks of where you land. No fall damage. 6 + 40% Might + 20% Toughness F Iron Skin 25 20 s Absorption for 10 s: 4 + 20% Toughness + 20% Fortitude health, rounded down to whole Absorption levels (4 health, 2 hearts, each). none Sneak + F Ultimate Unlocks with your subclass. Subclasses Subclass Upgrade Ultimate (Sneak + F) Paladin (holy hybrid) Consecrate replaces Ground Slam (25 mana, 8 s): a slam for 5 + 40% Might + 20% Vitality that blesses the ground for 4 s. Enemies on it take 2 + 10% Might + 10% Vitality every half second, and players on it get Regeneration II. Divine Judgment (50 mana, 40 s): holy light strikes every hostile mob within 10 blocks for 12 + 60% Might + 40% Vitality, and heals you and nearby players. Berserker (raw damage) Savage Slam replaces Crashing Slam (15 mana, 8 s): a harder Crashing Slam for 8 + 50% Might + 25% Ferocity. Landing gives you Strength I for 5 s. Bloodrage (40 mana, 40 s): an 8 s rage with Strength I and Speed I. Every melee hit deals 2 + 15% Might + 15% Ferocity bonus damage and heals you a little. Perk tree Every class tree has the same shape: a shared trunk and three branches, each ending in a keystone. How points, refunds and the Trades nodes work is on Skill trees . Small nodes give bonus stat levels. They work like gear bonuses: they can go past the cap, but they don't unlock perks. Might small nodes give +1 level instead of +2. Juggernaut The left branch, entered from Thick Hide. Small nodes: 4 × +2 Toughness, 3 × +2 Fortitude, 3 × +2 Vitality. Node Type Cost Effect Bulwark Notable 1 +1.5 armour. Stone Skin Notable 1 +1 armour toughness. Shield Master Notable 1 A monster's hit on your shield heals you 1 heart. Iron Resolve Notable 1 Iron Skin lasts 15 s (was 10). Iron Fortress ★ Keystone 3 With a shield in your offhand: no knockback, projectiles hit you for 30% less, and monster hits you take deal 3 damage to every monster within 4 blocks. -0.5 attack speed. Crashing Slam is gone from your keys. Berserker The middle branch, entered from Light Feet. Small nodes: 7 × +1 Might, 3 × +2 Ferocity. Node Type Cost Effect Brute Notable 1 +2 melee damage. Heavy Hitter Notable 1 +1 melee damage and +0.5 knockback on your hits. Reckless Notable 1 Ground Slam deals +40%; Iron Skin lasts half as long. Bloodthirst Notable 1 Your melee hits heal you 1 health. Berserker Oath ★ Keystone 3 +4 melee damage and +0.4 attack speed. -6 max health. Ground Slam costs 30 mana. Crusader The right branch, entered from Second Breath. Small nodes: 4 × +2 Vitality, 3 × +1 Might, 3 × +2 Steadfast. Node Type Cost Effect Lifeblood Notable 1 +6 max health. Hallowed Notable 1 Consecrate's holy ground lasts 6 s (was 4). Judgment Notable 1 Divine Judgment's cooldown is 30 s (was 40). Standard Bearer Notable 1 Allies within 8 blocks deal +10% melee damage. Titan's Grip ★ Keystone 3 +6 melee damage. -0.6 attack speed. Your slams knock mobs 2 blocks further. Good to know The Berserker branch and the Berserker subclass share a name, but you don't have to take both. Every branch works with every subclass. Hallowed and Judgment only help a Paladin. Iron Fortress also locks out Savage Slam, the Berserker's version of Crashing Slam. Reckless and Iron Resolve stack. Iron Skin then lasts about 8 s. Fortitude trains from monster hits you take with a shield in your offhand, Toughness from big hits (3+ hearts), Vitality from any damage you survive and Steadfast from hostile mobs hitting you. A Warrior who tanks levels up their defences fast. See Stats ."}, {"t": "Herald", "u": "herald.html", "c": "Classes & stats", "s": "The Herald is the support class, with a small healing hymn, banners and anthems that buff your party, and hexes that weaken enemies, scaling with Arcana, Vitality and Fortune.", "h": "Role Weapons and armour Abilities Subclasses Perk tree Choir Cursebinder Standard-Bearer Good to know", "x": "Role The Herald is the support class. It has one small heal. The rest of the kit raises your party and breaks your enemies : banners and anthems give nearby players real stat levels, and hexes strip enemies' armour and slow them. The Herald is the only class that has its ultimate from the start . A subclass swaps it for a different one. Role Support: party buffs, enemy debuffs, a small heal Casts with A Standard (the Herald’s Standard and other rolled Standards) Starter weapon Herald’s Standard Armour Gold-dyed leather looks. Herald armour rolls slightly less armour than a Warrior's (×0.95). Scales with (+25% training XP) Arcana, Vitality, Fortune Keys 4: Right-click, Sneak + Right-click, F, Sneak + F Subclasses (level 20) Hymnwarden, Warchanter Weapons and armour Standards are the Herald's class weapon. They come in many looks, from camp banners to bell poles. Only a Standard casts Herald abilities. Herald gear rolls mostly Arcana , Vitality and Fortune . It rarely rolls Might, Ferocity or Precision. See Weapons and Armour for the full lists. Abilities Buffs from banners and anthems are stat levels : \"+6 Might\" works exactly like 6 extra levels of Might while it lasts. Buffs reach every player in range, including you and your party . Hover an ability on the class page to see your numbers. See Abilities for how keys, mana and cooldowns work. Key Ability Mana Cooldown What it does Right-click Mending Hymn 20 8 s Heals you and every player within 6 blocks for 4 + 10% Vitality health and gives Regeneration I for 3 s. Also cleanses Poison and Wither. Sneak + Right-click Hex of Frailty 20 10 s Curses the spot you look at (24 blocks). Enemies within 4 blocks take 3 + 25% Arcana + 15% Fortune damage, and for 6 s get Weakness, Slowness, Glowing and -5 armour. F Rallying Banner 25 20 s Plant your banner for 10 s. Every player within 8 blocks of it is Rallied: +6 Might, +6 Precision, +6 Arcana and Speed I. The buff stays for 1 s after you step away. Sneak + F Anthem of the Stranded (ultimate) 50 40 s A 10 s anthem that moves with you (10 blocks). Players get Fortitude and Toughness, each 6 + 15% Arcana + 5% Vitality of yours (at most 20), plus +8 Steadfast and +4 Might, Precision, Arcana and Swiftness. Enemies in it get Slowness II, Weakness, Glowing and -5 armour. Subclasses Subclass Upgrade New ultimate (Sneak + F) Hymnwarden (protector) Warding Banner replaces Rallying Banner (25 mana, 20 s): plant a warding banner for 10 s. Players within 8 blocks get +8 Fortitude, +8 Steadfast and +4 Vitality, and 2 hearts of Absorption when it is planted. Aegis Chorus (50 mana, 40 s): an 8 s chorus around you (10 blocks). Players get Resistance I and Fortitude and Toughness, each 10 + 25% Arcana + 10% Vitality of yours (at most 30). Every second it strips Poison, Wither, Slowness, Weakness, Blindness, Darkness and Hunger. Warchanter (aggressor) Curse of Ruin replaces Hex of Frailty (22 mana, 10 s): curse the spot you look at (24 blocks). Enemies within 5 blocks take 5 + 35% Arcana + 20% Fortune and for 6 s get Wither I, Weakness II, Slowness II, Glowing and -8 armour. Battle Hymn (50 mana, 40 s): an 8 s war song around you (10 blocks). Players get +8 Might, +8 Precision, +8 Arcana, +6 Ferocity, Strength I and Speed I. Enemies in it get Weakness, Glowing and -5 armour. Mending Hymn stays your only heal in both subclasses. Perk tree Every class tree has the same shape: a shared trunk and three branches, each ending in a keystone. How points, refunds and the Trades nodes work is on Skill trees . Small nodes give bonus stat levels. They work like gear bonuses: they can go past the cap, but they don't unlock perks. Choir The left branch, entered from Thick Hide. Small nodes: 7 × +2 Vitality, 3 × +2 Arcana. Node Type Cost Effect Mana Font Notable 1 +40 max mana. Long Hymn Notable 1 Mending Hymn heals +2. Wide Banner Notable 1 Rallying Banner reaches 12 blocks (was 8). Encore Notable 1 Anthem of the Stranded lasts 14 s (was 10). Chorus of the Stranded ★ Keystone 3 Your banners and anthems buff you twice as much. Hex of Frailty does no damage. Cursebinder The middle branch, entered from Light Feet. Small nodes: 7 × +2 Arcana, 3 × +2 Fortune. Node Type Cost Effect Spellweaver Notable 1 +2 damage on every ability hit. Deep Hex Notable 1 Hex of Frailty lasts 8 s (was 6). Spreading Rot Notable 1 Curse of Ruin's radius is 7 (was 5). Mark of Ruin Notable 1 Hexed mobs take +10% damage from everyone. Dirge ★ Keystone 3 Every hex also deals 2 + 15% Arcana a second while it lasts. Mending Hymn heals half. Standard-Bearer The right branch, entered from Second Breath. Small nodes: 7 × +2 Vitality, 3 × +2 Fortune. Node Type Cost Effect Lifeblood Notable 1 +6 max health. Shield Master Notable 1 A monster's hit on your shield heals you 1 heart. Mana Tide Notable 1 +2 mana per second. Rally Notable 1 Planting a banner gives every player in it 1 heart of Absorption. Last Stand ★ Keystone 3 An ally within 8 blocks who falls under 20% health gets Resistance II for 3 s (once a minute each). -15% move speed. Good to know Encore also lengthens Aegis Chorus and Battle Hymn (8 s to 12 s), and Deep Hex also lengthens Curse of Ruin. Rally, Last Stand and every banner and anthem help the players around you, so a Herald shines in a party . The buffs show on your stats while they last, because they are real stat levels: armour, damage and health move with them. Vitality (your heal and anthems) trains when you take damage and survive. Arcana trains as you spend mana. Fortune trains from fresh loot chests, trades and enchanting. See Stats ."}, {"t": "Skill trees", "u": "skill-trees.html", "c": "Classes & stats", "s": "How the class perk trees work, including perk points, the trunk, the three branches, notables and keystones, the shared Trades nodes, refunds and resets.", "h": "The perk tree The shape Node types How far points go Each class's branches The Trades Refunds and resets", "x": "The perk tree Every class has its own perk tree . You spend perk points on it to shape your build: more damage for one ability, a tougher body, faster mana, or a keystone that changes how you play. Open it with [✦ Perk Tree] on your character sheet (right-click your Exile’s Journal ), or with [Passive Tree] in the level-up message. Points: you get 1 perk point per character level (see Leveling ). This is on top of the attribute point each level gives you for the stat sheet . Before you pick a class you only see the bottom row (the Origin and the shared Trades nodes). Any points you don't spend wait for your class tree. The tree icons come from the resource pack. The shape Every class tree is drawn the same way. Only the names and effects change: K-o-o-o-N N-o-K-o-N N-o-o-o-K | | | | N-o-N-o-o o-N-o-N-o o-o-N-o-N | | | | | | | o-o-E-o-N o-o-E-o-o N-o-E-o-o | | | o---------o---------o | o | N-N-N-N-N-N-S-N-N-N-N S, the Origin , sits on the bottom row. You always own it. The trunk climbs from the Origin. Every class has the same trunk: Node Type Effect Steady Breath small +20 max mana. Light Feet small (the hub) +4% move speed. Thick Hide small (left of the hub) +4 max health. Second Breath notable (right of the hub) +25% mana regeneration. The three branches are the grids on top. Each is 3 rows of 5 nodes. The left branch is entered from Thick Hide, the middle from Light Feet and the right from Second Breath, always through the bottom-middle node (E). Inside a branch there are several routes and loops. It holds 4 notables, and the keystone sits in the far top corner, six steps from the entry . The Trades run left and right of the Origin on the bottom row (see below). Node types Symbol Type Cost What it gives ● Small 1 point +2 bonus levels in one of the branch's stats ( +1 for Might). Like gear bonuses, these can go past the cap of 50 but don't unlock perks or passives. ◆ Notable 1 point A named effect, often an upgrade to one of your abilities. ★ Keystone 3 points A big build-defining effect with a real downside. You can take any node that touches a node you own . How far points go One branch is 15 nodes and costs 17 points (10 small, 4 notables, 1 keystone). The trunk costs 4. The fastest keystone is in the middle branch: 2 trunk nodes + 6 branch nodes + the 3-point keystone = 11 points , so character level 12 (you earn your first point at level 2). A side branch needs one more trunk node (level 13). By level 40 you have 39 points: the trunk and about two full branches. All three branches take 55 points, so for most of your climb you have to choose. Each class's branches Class Left (from Thick Hide) Middle (from Light Feet) Right (from Second Breath) Mage Battlemage ★ Arcane Barrage Elementalist ★ Overcharge Mastery Warden ★ Blood Magic Archer Rain ★ Rain of Ruin Marksman ★ Deadeye Focus Skirmisher ★ Parting Shot Rogue Assassin ★ Cutthroat Blade Dancer ★ Thousand Cuts Shadow ★ Ghost Warrior Juggernaut ★ Iron Fortress Berserker ★ Berserker Oath Crusader ★ Titan's Grip Herald Choir ★ Chorus of the Stranded Cursebinder ★ Dirge Standard-Bearer ★ Last Stand Every node and its exact text is on the class pages. The Trades The Trades are shared by every class and kept when you change class. They form two chains that start next to the Origin. Each node costs 1 point, and you take them in order outwards from the Origin. You can buy Trades nodes even before you have a class. Gathering chain (to the left of the Origin): Order Node Effect 1 Green Hands +10% bonus chance on every gathering skill. 2 Lucky Find +1 luck. 3 Stonecutter Haste I while holding a pickaxe. 4 Angler's Luck +2 luck while holding a fishing rod. 5 Bountiful Life Double training XP for Mining, Logging, Farming, Fishing and Cooking. 6 Master Chef Eating cooked food gives a random 30 s buff: Speed, Haste, Strength or Regeneration. Blacksmithing chain (to the right of the Origin): Order Node Effect 1 Forge Hand +1 gem roll on every piece you forge. 2 Steady Hammer +1 affix slot on every piece you forge. 3 Thrifty Combining at the forge takes 8, not 9. 4 Quick Kiln Blacksmithing XP +25%. See Forge for what gem rolls and affix slots mean. Refunds and resets Taking back one node is free , but only a node at the end of your path (one that touches just one other node you own). That way nothing you own gets cut off. In a loop, you unpick from one end. Click an owned node to refund it. [Reset tree] refunds your whole class tree and your Trades. It costs 25 gold coins per character level (level 20 = 500 coins). It is free while you own only one node. Changing class refunds every point in your class tree for free . The new class starts with an empty tree, and your Trades nodes stay. See Classes . The stat sheet's [↺ Respec] is a different thing: it refunds attribute points, not perk points. See Stats ."}, {"t": "Abilities", "u": "abilities.html", "c": "Classes & stats", "s": "How class abilities work, including casting keys, the ability bar, mana, cooldowns, damage scaling, and moving abilities between keys.", "h": "Casting The ability bar Reading an ability Mana Cooldowns Moving your abilities Weapons with their own skill Stats, perks and nodes that help abilities", "x": "Casting Once you have a class , you cast abilities while holding your class weapon . Each ability sits on a key : Key Mage, Rogue, Warrior, Herald Archer 1 Right-click Sneak + start drawing your bow 2 Sneak + Right-click F (swap hands) 3 F (swap hands) Sneak + F 4 Sneak + F: your ultimate - Ultimates unlock with your subclass at character level 20. The Herald is the exception: it has an ultimate from the start. An Archer's ultimate replaces Piercing Shot on Sneak + F. Warriors cannot block with a shield. Their right-click always casts. Each class's abilities are listed on its page: Mage , Archer , Rogue , Warrior , Herald . The ability bar While you hold your class weapon, the bar above your hotbar shows your mana and every key: green name: ready. blue name: not enough mana. grey name with red seconds : on cooldown. Out of combat, training XP pop-ups take over the bar for a moment. Then your abilities come back. Reading an ability Hover an ability's name on the class page. The hover shows its mana cost, its cooldown (after your cooldown reductions), its damage formula and the damage it deals with your stats right now . Damage = base + a share of your stat levels. \"8 + 50% Arcana + 30% Precision\" with Arcana 30 and Precision 20 deals 8 + 15 + 6 = 29 . Your total stat level counts, including bonus levels from gear, the tree, amulets and buffs. See Stats . Ability damage: - only hits hostile mobs . Animals, villagers and other players are never hurt. - is credited to you, so kills count as yours. - is not blocked by a monster's hit cooldown, so several abilities can land on the same monster at once. Mana Everyone uses one mana pool: Max mana = 100 + 4 per Arcana level. More comes from Deep Reserves (+30), Mana Font (+40), Steady Breath (+20) and some gear and attributes. It refills 5% of your max every second (at least 1). Mana Flow and Second Breath each add +25%, Quick Recovery adds +1 a second and Mana Tide +2 a second. Spending mana trains Arcana : 1 training XP per 5 mana. Cooldowns A cooldown belongs to the ability , not the key, so moving an ability never resets it. Shorter cooldowns: Archmage (Arcana level 50 perk) cuts ability cooldowns by 15% and Quick Wit (Mage tree) by 10%. Some notables shorten one ability, such as Rapid Bolt, Judgment and Spring Heels. Moving your abilities You can put any ability you have earned on any key. Open the class page (from your character sheet) and find Your keys : [change] next to a key lists your whole pool : your class's three abilities, your subclass's upgrade, your ultimate and the ultimate of a set you wear (see Sets ). Abilities you haven't earned yet are greyed out and say why. Picking an ability that is already on another key swaps the two . – Nothing empties a key. [⟲ Default keys] puts everything back. Only one ultimate (yours or a set's) can be on your keys at a time. When your subclass upgrades an ability, the upgrade takes the old ability's key by default. The old one stays in your pool with its own cooldown, so you can run both. You can't change keys for 10 seconds after taking damage . The page tells you how long is left. Changing class or subclass puts your keys back to default. Weapons with their own skill A weapon with a socketed skill gem (see Gems ), or one of the class signature weapons , casts its own skill on key 1 while you hold it. This is Right-click, or Sneak + draw for an Archer. Whatever you put on key 1 waits until you put that weapon away. Signature weapons have their own cooldown. Stats, perks and nodes that help abilities Source Effect Arcana perks Mana Flow (+25% mana regen, level 10), Spell Echo (10% chance an ability costs no mana, level 20), Arcane Mastery (+3 damage on every ability hit, level 40), Archmage (cooldowns 15% shorter, level 50). Arcana passives Tier I (pick one): Deep Reserves, Quick Recovery, Spell Siphon (kills restore 10 mana). Tier II (pick one): Battle Mage (melee hits restore 3 mana), Arcane Surge (+5 ability damage above 80% mana), Ley Ward. Tree Steady Breath, Second Breath, Spellweaver (+2 damage on every ability hit), Quick Wit, Mana Font, Mana Tide, Overcharge. The full perk and passive lists are on Stats ."}, {"t": "Stats", "u": "stats.html", "c": "Classes & stats", "s": "All 16 stats, with what each level gives, the level cap, how each one trains, the perks they unlock, and the passives you choose.", "h": "How stats work The 16 stats Training speed Perks Passives Respec", "x": "How stats work Exile RPG has 16 stats . Each one has its own level and levels up by doing its thing . You get stronger at what you actually do: fight in melee and Might rises, sprint and Swiftness rises, mine and Mining rises. Level = trained levels + attribute points you spent on it , up to the cap of 50 . Attribute points: every character level gives you 1 attribute point . Spend it on any stat with [+1] or [+5] on the character sheet or on the stat's own page. A spent point is +1 level right away. (You also get 1 perk point per character level, for your perk tree .) Bonus levels (shown in aqua as +N ) come from gear, sets, amulets, your perk tree and Herald buffs. They add to the stat's effect and can go past the cap . They don't unlock perks or passives and don't count towards prestige . What a stat does = (level + bonus levels) × its per-level value. Stat effects, perks and passives only apply while you hold a real weapon (sword, axe, mace, trident, bow, crossbow or Exile RPG weapon). Bare-handed or holding a tool, you punch for exactly 1 heart. Open your character sheet by right-clicking your Exile’s Journal . Each stat has one row with its level, bonus levels, prestiges (✦), an XP bar, [+1] / [+5] when you have points, ★ when a passive is ready to choose, and MAX [✦] at the cap. Click a stat's name to open its page: its effect, how to train it, its perks and its passives. [✚ Bonuses] lists every bonus you have and where it comes from. The 16 stats Stat Each level gives At level 50 Trains by Base training XP ❤ Vitality +1 max health (half a heart) +50 health (25 hearts) Taking damage and surviving 2 per point of damage taken ⚔ Might +0.4 melee damage +20 melee damage Dealing melee damage 1 per point of melee damage. Needs 1.5× the XP per level ⚡ Ferocity +0.04 attack speed +2.0 attack speed Killing mobs 5 per kill ➶ Precision +2% damage on arrows you shoot +100% arrow damage Hitting mobs with arrows 6 per arrow hit ✧ Arcana +4 max mana; class abilities scale with it +200 max mana Spending mana on abilities, enchanting 1 per 5 mana spent, 5 per enchantment ⛨ Fortitude +0.2 armour +10 armour Taking monster hits with a shield in your offhand 2 per point of damage ◆ Toughness +0.15 armour toughness (less damage from big hits) +7.5 armour toughness Taking big hits (3+ hearts) 10 per big hit ➹ Swiftness +0.5% move speed +25% move speed Sprinting 1 per 10 blocks ⚓ Steadfast +1.5% knockback resistance +75% knockback resistance Getting hit by hostile mobs 3 per hit ✦ Fortune +0.2 luck (better loot rolls) +10 luck Looting fresh chests, trading with villagers, enchanting 25 per fresh chest, 6 per trade, 10 per enchantment ⛏ Mining +0.4 mining speed with the right tool +20 mining speed Mining stone and ores 1 per stone-type block, 5 per ore. Trains at half pace ♣ Logging +1% chance a chopped log drops twice 50% Chopping logs 3 per log. Trains at half pace ☘ Farming +1% chance a harvested crop drops an extra one 50% Harvesting crops 2 per crop (capped per minute) ≈ Fishing +1% chance to reel in a second catch 50% Catching fish 10 per catch ♨ Cooking +1% chance cooked food comes out doubled 50% Cooking or crafting food 3 per dish ⚒ Blacksmithing Mastery: the rarity you forge and the best metal you may use Legendary (netherite) Forging gear at an anvil per forge. Trains 25% slower Blacksmithing mastery: levels 1-10 forge Common (copper), 11-21 Uncommon (iron), 22-32 Rare (gold), 33-43 Mythic (diamond), 44-50 Legendary (netherite). Forge ingredients drop from mining, logging, fishing and hunting, and modifiers from monsters and ores. See Forge . Gathering bonuses (Logging, Farming, Fishing, Cooking) roll per item. The chance equals the stat's total level in %, so gear bonus levels count too. Training speed The base training XP above is then adjusted: Combat stats train at half speed: Vitality, Might, Ferocity, Precision, Arcana, Fortitude, Toughness and Steadfast. Every stat except the five gathering stats and Blacksmithing (that is, the eight combat stats plus Swiftness and Fortune) also earns only two thirds of its XP. Mining and Logging train at half pace . Blacksmithing trains 25% slower . Your class's scaling stats train 25% faster (see Classes ). Prestige multiplies a stat's training XP: ×2 after one prestige, ×3 after two, and so on (see Prestige ). Some nodes and passives double training XP: Bountiful Life (tree) for all five gathering stats, and the Geologist, Arborist, Green Thumb, Angler, Gourmand and Apprentice passives for their own stat. XP needed for the next level = 20 + 8 × your current level. Level 0 to 1 needs 20 XP, and 49 to 50 needs 412. Might needs 1.5 times as much: 30 + 12 × level. Leftover fractions are never lost. Small gains add up until they make a whole point. Training XP from every stat except the gathering stats and Blacksmithing also feeds your character level . See Leveling . Perks Each stat unlocks 4 perks automatically at levels 10, 20, 40 and 50 . Only trained levels and spent points count. Gear bonus levels don't. Stat Level 10 Level 20 Level 40 Level 50 Vitality Recovery : After 8s without taking damage you regenerate 1 health per second. Second Wind : Below 30% health you get Regeneration II for 5s (60s cooldown). Thick Blooded : Immune to Poison and Hunger. Titan Heart : +10% max health. Might Heavy Blows : Your melee hits knock enemies back further. Bloodlust : Kills give Strength I for 4s. Brutal Force : +10% melee damage. Warlord : Kills heal you for 2 hearts. Ferocity Adrenaline : Kills give Absorption I for 6s (10s cooldown). Frenzy : Kills give Speed I for 4s. Relentless : Permanent Haste I. Rampage : 3 kills in quick succession give Strength II for 6s. Precision Steady Aim : Arrows shot while sneaking deal +10% damage. Swift Shot : Your arrows fly 15% faster (more range and damage). Hamstring : Every 4th arrow you shoot (ability arrows count) is a hamstring arrow: what it hits is slowed (Slowness II 2 s) and weakened (Weakness I 3 s). Deadeye : Arrows have a 15% chance to deal 50% more damage. Arcana Mana Flow : +25% mana regeneration. Spell Echo : Abilities have a 10% chance to cost no mana. Arcane Mastery : +3 damage on every ability hit. Archmage : Ability cooldowns are 15% shorter. Fortitude Iron Skin : +1 armor. Shield Wall : With a shield in your offhand, arrows and other projectiles that hit you deal 30% less. Riposte : Being hit by a monster with a shield in your offhand gives Strength I for 3s. Aegis : Being hit by a monster with a shield in your offhand heals 2 hearts (5s cooldown). Toughness Hard Landing : -25% fall damage. Unshaken : Taking a big hit gives Absorption I for 6s (30s cooldown). Juggernaut : Immune to Slowness, Weakness and Mining Fatigue. Colossus : +1 armor toughness and +50% explosion knockback resistance. Swiftness Light Feet : You walk up full blocks without jumping. Fleet Footed : Speed I while sprinting. Dolphin Kick : Dolphin’s Grace while in water. Windrunner : +10% jump height and 3 more blocks before fall damage. Steadfast Rally : When a hostile mob hits you, 15% chance of Speed I and Strength I for 3s. Rooted : +30% knockback resistance while sneaking. Iron Will : Immune to Nausea, Blindness and Darkness. Immovable : No knockback at all while sneaking. Fortune Treasure Sense : Opening a fresh loot chest also gives a burst of experience. Golden Touch : Kills have a 10% chance to drop 1-3 gold nuggets. Haggler : Villagers treat you as a Hero of the Village (cheaper trades). Midas : +1 luck, and kills have a 2% chance to drop an emerald. Mining Prospector : Ores you mine have a 25% chance to drop bonus experience. Night Miner : Night vision while below Y 0. Vein Luck : Ores have an 8% chance to drop an extra ore. Deep Delver : Haste II while below Y 0. Logging Woodsman : Haste I while holding an axe. Apple Picker : Chopped logs have a 5% chance to drop an apple. Double Chop : Your bonus logs come in twos. Heartwood : Chopped logs have a 1% chance to drop a golden apple. Farming Seed Saver : Wheat and beetroot have a 15% chance to drop extra seeds. Farmhand : Speed I while walking on farmland. Bountiful : Your bonus harvests come in twos. Golden Harvest : Crops have a 2% chance to drop a golden carrot. Fishing Patient Angler : +1 luck while holding a fishing rod (better catches). Fresh Catch : Catching a fish gives Regeneration II for 2s. Treasure Hunter : Each catch has a 5% chance of a bonus treasure. Kraken Favor : Conduit Power while in water. Cooking Hearty Meal : Eating cooked food gives Regeneration I for 4s. Well Fed : Eating cooked food gives Absorption I for 20s. Master Chef : Your bonus dishes come in twos. Gourmet : Eating cooked food gives Strength I for 10s. Blacksmithing Careful Hands : Modifiers never fail when forged in. Reclaimer : [Reclaim held piece] at the forge turns a piece you forged back into 1-3 of its ingots. Masters Mark : Forged pieces carry your name and +1 to every affix roll. Legend Smith : One extra modifier slot (7). Passives Each stat also offers passives that you choose . At level 15 (Tier I) and level 35 (Tier II) you pick one of three on the stat's page. A ★ on the sheet means a choice is waiting. Stat Tier Choice 1 Choice 2 Choice 3 Vitality I (15) Hardy : +4 max health. Mending : Regenerate about 1 health every 6 seconds. Vital Surge : Kills heal you about 1.5 hearts. II (35) Colossal Heart : +8 max health. Undying : Dropping below 25% health gives Resistance I for 5s (90s cooldown). Lifeleech : Melee hits on hostile mobs heal you a little. Might I (15) Brawler : +1 melee damage. Bully : Your melee hits knock enemies back further. Sweeper : +50% sweeping attack damage. II (35) Titan Grip : +2.5 melee damage. Crushing Blows : Melee hits slow enemies (Slowness I, 2s). Concussive Blows : Melee hits have a 15% chance to stun (Slowness IV and Weakness II, 2s). Ferocity I (15) Quick Hands : +0.2 attack speed. Bloodthirst : Kills give Haste I for 5s. Predator : Speed I while a hostile mob is within 8 blocks. II (35) Battle Trance : +0.4 attack speed. Carnage : Kills give Speed II and Jump Boost II for 3s. Whirling Fury : Strength I while 3 or more hostile mobs are within 6 blocks. Precision I (15) Eagle Eye : +10% arrow damage. Quiver Saver : 25% chance to get your arrow back when you shoot. Hunter Mark : Arrow hits make the target glow for 5s. II (35) Sharpshooter : +25% arrow damage. Pinning Shot : Arrow hits slow the target (Slowness II, 2s). Crippling Shot : Arrow hits have a 25% chance to weaken the target (Weakness I, 4s). Arcana I (15) Deep Reserves : +30 max mana. Quick Recovery : +1 mana per second. Spell Siphon : Kills restore 10 mana. II (35) Battle Mage : Melee hits on hostile mobs restore 3 mana. Arcane Surge : +5 ability damage while above 80% mana. Ley Ward : Taking a big hit spends 20 mana to give Absorption II for 5s. Fortitude I (15) Thick Plating : +1 armor. Shield Bearer : Being hit by a monster with a shield in your offhand gives Speed I for 3s. Bracer : Being hit by a monster with a shield in your offhand heals you a little. II (35) Bastion : +2 armor. Spiked Shield : Being hit by a monster with a shield in your offhand deals 3 damage to hostile mobs within 4 blocks. Stalwart : +2 armor while sneaking. Toughness I (15) Tough Hide : +0.5 armor toughness. Fire Hardened : You burn for half as long. Blast Proof : Explosions barely push you back. II (35) Bedrock : +1.5 armor toughness. Pain Eater : Taking a big hit gives Regeneration II for 3s. Thorned Hide : Hostile mobs that hit you take 2 damage. Swiftness I (15) Fleet : +5% move speed. Sneak Step : Sneak 50% faster. Swimmer : Move faster in water. II (35) Gale : +10% move speed. Spring Heels : Jump higher and fall 2 more blocks without damage. Momentum : Sprinting for 3 seconds gives Speed II. Steadfast I (15) Sure Footed : +10% knockback resistance. Brace : Getting hit by a hostile mob gives 1 heart of Absorption (+½ heart per 10 Steadfast) for 4s (10s cooldown). Grounded : Immune to Levitation. II (35) Bulwark Stance : +20% knockback resistance. Last Stand : While 3 or more hostile mobs are within 6 blocks, heal ½ heart (+½ per 25 Steadfast) every 2s. Earthbind : While sneaking, hostile mobs within 4 blocks get Slowness I. Fortune I (15) Lucky : +1 luck. Scavenger : Kills have a 10% chance to give an arrow, string or bone. Gilded : Kills drop a little extra experience. II (35) Fortunate : +2 luck. Jackpot : Kills have a 1% chance to give a diamond. Gold Rush : Kills have an 8% chance to give a gold ingot. Mining I (15) Quarryman : Break blocks 15% faster. Aqua Miner : Mine much faster underwater. Geologist : Double Mining training XP. II (35) Excavator : Break blocks 30% faster. Gem Sense : Mining turns up forge gems and modifiers half again as often. Ore Luck : Ores have a 6% chance to drop an extra ore. Logging I (15) Lumberjack : Break blocks 30% faster while holding an axe. Charcoal Burner : Logs have a 6% chance to drop charcoal. Arborist : Double Logging training XP. II (35) Feller : Break blocks 60% faster while holding an axe. Axe Mastery : +2 melee damage while holding an axe. Reforest : Logs have a 5% chance to drop an oak sapling. Farming I (15) Green Thumb : Double Farming training XP. Composter : Crops have a 6% chance to drop bone meal. Baker : Crops have a 3% chance to drop bread. II (35) Harvester : +10% bonus harvest chance. Shepherd : Farm animals within 8 blocks of you slowly regenerate. Golden Fields : Crops have a 2% chance to drop a glistering melon slice. Fishing I (15) Angler : Double Fishing training XP. Gills : Breathe longer underwater. Lucky Lure : +2 luck while holding a fishing rod (better catches). II (35) Aquatic : Swim much faster. Sea Treasures : Catches have a 4% chance to bring up a nautilus shell. Deep Sight : Night Vision while your head is underwater. Cooking I (15) Gourmand : Double Cooking training XP. Energizing : Eating cooked food gives Speed I for 10s. Iron Gut : Eating cooked food cures Poison, Hunger and Nausea. II (35) Banquet : +10% bonus dish chance. Feast Fuel : Eating cooked food gives Haste I for 30s. Comfort Food : Eating cooked food gives 2 hearts of Absorption for 30s and +2 saturation. Blacksmithing I (15) Keen Eye : Ingredients drop 50% more often from gathering. Salvager : Modifiers drop 50% more often from monsters. Apprentice : Double Blacksmithing training XP. II (35) Spark Saver : Recipes need one ingot less (never below 1). Gem Setter : Gem modifiers count one tier higher when forged in. Keen Edge : Forged weapons get +1 to every affix roll. Respec [↺ Respec] on the character sheet (or Warden Aldric's \"Respec attributes\") refunds every attribute point you spent . It costs 25 gold coins per character level . Your trained levels and passive choices are kept. Perks you only had thanks to spent points go away until you reach those levels again. This only resets attribute points. Your perk tree is reset separately (see Skill trees )."}, {"t": "Attributes", "u": "attributes.html", "c": "Classes & stats", "s": "Your Personal Attribute is one random trait rolled when you first join. This page covers the four tiers, every attribute, and how to awaken or reroll yours.", "h": "What an attribute is Tiers Awakening Rerolling: the Orb of Fate Every attribute Common Rare Legendary Trash", "x": "What an attribute is Every exile has one Personal Attribute : a trait rolled at random the first time you join the world. It stays with you and shapes your build. It might be extra armour, lightning on your hits, a double jump, or a drawback you learn to live with. Don't confuse it with attribute points , the points you spend on your stats each level. The two are separate. You see your attribute in the Attribute row of your character sheet (right-click your Exile’s Journal ). Hover the row for the attribute card. Click it for the attribute page. The card shows the base effect, the awakened form and your Awakening Deed progress bar. Many attribute numbers grow with your character level or stats, and the card shows the values you have right now. The tables below give the starting numbers. Tiers Tier Colour Chance when rolled Attributes Trash grey 25% 7 Common white 45% 9 Rare aqua 24% 6 Legendary gold 6% 3 Awakening Every attribute has an awakened form . Your attribute keeps its base effect and adds the awakened one (sometimes it replaces a drawback). There are three ways to awaken: Complete its Awakening Deed , at any level. The deed always matches the attribute's theme, for example \"Take 5000 damage\" for Thick Skin. Reach character level 30. Any attribute that isn't awakened yet awakens by itself. Use an Awakening Stone. Right-click it to awaken your attribute at once, at any level. Awakening is announced to the whole world. Rerolling: the Orb of Fate An Orb of Fate rerolls your attribute. Right-click it, then confirm with [Yes, reroll] . The new attribute can be of any tier. All awakening progress is lost , so think before you roll. Awakening Stones and Orbs of Fate are very rare drops from bosses . Every attribute Common Attribute Base effect Awakened form Awakening Deed Thick Skin +2 armour. Hide of the Old Camp : +4 armour, -10% damage from monsters. Take 5000 damage Long Arms +0.5 reach. Reach of the Reaper : +1 reach; hits from 3.5+ blocks deal +15%. Land 500 hits from 3+ blocks Quick Hands +10% attack speed. Blur : +20% attack speed; every 10th hit resets the cooldown of the ability on your first key. Land 3000 hits Hearty +10% max health. Oak Heart : +20% max health, and Regeneration I after 5 s out of combat. Heal 2000 health Keen Eye 10% of your melee hits are critical (+50% damage). Hawk’s Eye : 15% crits, and crits give Slowness I 2 s. Land 500 crits Night Owl Night Vision; +10% melee damage at night. Moonlit : Speed I at night. Kill 200 monsters at night Forager Gathering XP +25%; crops x2. Bountiful : Ores x2 too. Gather 1000 times Steady Hand Bows and crossbows +15% damage. Dead Eye : Your arrows pierce one target. Land 300 arrow hits from 15+ blocks Mana Well +20% max mana. Deep Well : +40% max mana and +50% mana regeneration. Cast 500 abilities Rare Attribute Base effect Awakened form Awakening Deed Berserker’s Blood Below 50% health: +25% melee damage. Red Mist : +40% instead, and 10% lifesteal below 50%. Kill 300 monsters below 50% health Shadow Walker Sneak still for 2 s: Invisibility until you move or hit. Unseen : Sneak-walking keeps it; a monster you hit from it loses you for 5 s. Kill 100 monsters while invisible Stormborn 10% of melee hits chain lightning to 3 others within 4. Thunderhead : 25%, and chained targets get Slowness I. Chain 200 times Hollow Bones Slow Falling always; Jump Boost I. Wind Rider : A double jump (sneak mid-air). Fall 2000 blocks safely Mending Regeneration I after 5 s out of combat. Ward : It heals allies within 4 too. Heal 3000 (you and allies) Warlord Allies within 8 deal +10% melee damage. Banner : Allies get +20% while you are above 50% health, and every 10 s you get Absorption yourself, always. Allies near you kill 500 monsters Legendary Attribute Base effect Awakened form Awakening Deed Bloodline Melee hits heal 5% of the damage you deal; kills give 1 heart of Absorption (30 s). Sanguine Crown : 10% lifesteal, and the Absorption from kills stacks up to 4 hearts. Heal 500 health from your hits Hercules Might +10 levels; melee hits knock monsters back 3 blocks. Titan’s Grip : Right-click a monster (not a boss) to pick it up and throw it 8 blocks (it and what it hits take damage). Knock back 1000 monsters Reaper Kills heal you; a monster under 20% health has a 5% chance to die to any hit (not bosses). Harvest : 15%, and each reap gives a Soul (max 10, +2% melee damage each, lost on death). Reap 500 times Trash Trash attributes start with a drawback. Awakening one softens the drawback or adds an upside. Attribute Base effect Awakened form Awakening Deed Weak Stomach Food gives half its saturation, and 1 meal in 5 makes you sick (Nausea 5 s). Cast-Iron Gut : Poison and Hunger last half as long; Rotten Flesh and Spider Eyes no longer harm you. Eat 300 meals Heavy Bones -10% movement speed; +50% fall damage; immune to knockback. Set in Stone : -5% speed instead; +4 armour; immune to Slowness. Land 200 falls of 4+ blocks Glass Jaw +20% damage taken from melee. Thin Skin : Still +20%, but every melee hit you take gives a Spite stack (+3% damage dealt, 5 stacks, 10 s). Take 500 melee hits Coward’s Legs -5% damage dealt while above 80% health; Speed I only while below 30%. Second Wind : Below 30% health Speed II and Regeneration I; the damage penalty is gone. Sprint 5000 blocks Butterfingers 5% of your melee hits fumble: a 1-second disarm (you cannot attack). Juggler : The fumble is now a throw: your weapon flies 6 blocks for 150% damage and comes back; no disarm. Land 1000 melee hits Cursed Luck Fortune XP halved; loot rolls worse (one rarity step down 10% of the time); you never find skill gems. Jinx : Champions within 8 of you lose one modifier, and your loot no longer rolls worse. Open 50 loot chests Soot Hands Blacksmithing XP from the forge -25%; the tool or weapon in your hand loses durability twice as fast. Coal-Black Palms : The XP penalty is gone; fire and lava do you half damage. Forge 20 pieces"}, {"t": "Prestige", "u": "prestige.html", "c": "Classes & stats", "s": "Prestige a stat at level 50 to reset it to 0 in exchange for permanently faster training and a Legendary token.", "h": "What prestige is How to prestige What happens Should you prestige?", "x": "What prestige is When a stat reaches the cap of 50 , you can prestige it. The stat goes back to level 0, and from then on it trains much faster. Prestige is how you keep a stat growing after the cap, and how you show you've mastered it. How to prestige Get the stat to level 50 from trained levels and spent attribute points . Bonus levels from gear, sets, amulets and your perk tree don't count . On the character sheet the stat's row shows MAX [✦] . You can also open the stat's page and click [Prestige ✦] . Confirm with [Yes, prestige] . What happens The stat Goes back to level 0 and its XP is cleared. Spent attribute points Refunded , so you can spend them again anywhere. Perks That stat's perks have to be earned again as it levels back up. Passive choices That stat's Tier I and Tier II choices are cleared. Choose again at levels 15 and 35. Training speed +100% training XP for that stat, stacking: ×2 after one prestige, ×3 after two, ×4 after three, and so on. Reward +1 Legendary token . Your character sheet shows how many you have. Bonus levels from gear and the tree stay. They still add to the stat's effect while it climbs back up. Your sheet shows each stat's prestiges as ✦ , and the [✚ Bonuses] screen lists every prestige with its XP multiplier. Should you prestige? Your character level isn't slowed down. The share of training XP that feeds your character level is worked out before the prestige multiplier. Prestiging doesn't speed up character levels, and it doesn't slow them either. A capped stat still helps your character level a little. A stat at 50 earns no stat XP, but it still pays a small share (10%) of what it would have earned towards your character level. You lose power for a while. Until it climbs back, the prestiged stat gives less, and its perks are gone. Prestige when you can afford the dip: between big fights, or when your gear bonus levels carry the stat. Faster every time. Each prestige makes the next climb quicker, so a stat you use all the time (Might for a Warrior, Arcana for a Mage, Mining for a miner) climbs back fastest. See Stats for how each stat trains, and Leveling for character levels."}, {"t": "Gear", "u": "gear.html", "c": "Gear & forge", "s": "How gear works in Exile RPG - equipment slots, item levels, who loot is rolled for, and how often monsters drop it.", "h": "Gear at a glance Equipment slots Reading a tooltip Item level and the level lock Who loot is for How often monsters drop gear What kind of piece drops Very rare drops Other ways to get gear Selling See also", "x": "Gear at a glance Exile RPG gear is more than vanilla swords and armour. Every piece has a rarity , an item level , base numbers (damage, attack speed, armour) that grow with that level, and stat affixes that add bonus levels to your stats . You get gear in three main ways: Monster drops , rolled for you when something dies. Rewards : dungeon chests, bounties and quests . The forge : you make it yourself with Blacksmithing. Equipment slots A piece only counts in its own slot. A helmet held in your hand gives you nothing. Slot What goes there Main hand Weapons: swords, axes, maces, daggers, bows, staves, Mage Blades, standards Off hand Shields and a few special off-hand items Head, chest, legs, feet Armour Amulet slots (3) Amulets : the three slots in the right-hand column of your main inventory Your gear is checked a few times a second. Your stats update whenever you change what you wear or hold. Reading a tooltip From top to bottom, a gear tooltip shows: [Rarity] Name , coloured by rarity . Base name (on Rares and up), type and slot, e.g. Stiletto · Dagger · Main Hand . Level N · usable from character level N . Made for (armour only): the class the piece was rolled for. A bow's Draw Power (+% arrow damage). The item's skill, if it has one: a signature ability, an active or passive skill, a set bonus or set ultimate. Its affixes in their sockets (◆ filled, ◇ open), how many times it was rerolled, its flavour text and lore, and the marks of any orbs used on it. Item level and the level lock Every piece has an item level. A piece works only once your character level is at least its item level. Below that level: Weapons deal no damage. Bows' arrows do nothing. Nothing casts from the weapon: no class abilities, skill gems, signature skills or item actives. No mana or cooldown is spent. Armour, shields and amulets give no armour and no stats. They also don't count toward set bonuses. The first time you equip a locked piece, a chat line tells you which level it needs. While you hold a locked weapon, your ability bar shows the warning. Gear never expires: once a piece works for you, it keeps working at every level after. Higher item levels mean bigger base numbers. A sword's damage, for example, comes from its item level plus its rarity, so a fresh drop at your level usually beats an old piece. See weapons and armour for the numbers. Who loot is for Gear is rolled for one player at the moment it drops , and then it flies to that player: The killer gets the first piece. Any further pieces go round the other players nearby in turn (within 24 blocks, or 48 for bosses). The piece's item level is the receiver's level, or up to 3 below it . A level 20 player gets level 17, 18, 19 or 20 drops. Armour is made for the receiver's class 70% of the time and for another class 30% of the time, so you will sometimes find a piece for a friend. The floating label over a dropped piece shows its rarity, its name, its level and who it was rolled for. Anyone can pick it up and trade it. When you're alone, loot sprays out in a ring around the body. In a group, each piece flies to the player it was made for. Each dropped item also shows a coloured beam for its rarity. How often monsters drop gear Monsters come in ranks: Normal , Magic , Rare and Champion . Mini-bosses and bosses rank above those. Monster Gear drop Orb drop Normal 1.5% 1% Magic 7.5% 5% Rare 50% 20% Champion always always Mini-boss / boss always 1 piece, plus a 50% chance of one more for each extra player nearby always Each gear drop then rolls its rarity from a table based on the monster's level. Tougher monsters roll as if they were higher level. That table, and the chance that a drop rolls nothing at all, are on the rarity page. Some drops are not rolled gear but uniques or set pieces : a few older sets and named items such as Emberbrand, Ghostfang and Owleye Cowl. When a drop rolls Common, Uncommon or Rare, the chance of a named item instead is: Monster Chance of a unique or set piece Normal 5% Magic 10% Rare 20% Champion and up 50% What kind of piece drops If you have a class, about 45% of rolled drops are weapons, 10% are shields and 45% are armour. Of the weapons, 70% are your own class's type and 30% can be any class's. If you have no class yet (classes open at level 5), every kind is equally likely. Weapon bases also have a level band : the stronger bases only start dropping once you reach their band (see weapons ). Very rare drops On top of the normal drop, Rare monsters and Champions have a tiny chance of a Legendary weapon , and Champions an even smaller chance of an Extraordinary or God's Tool weapon . Monsters also drop forge ingredients and modifiers . Other ways to get gear Dungeon reward chests roll the rarity table as if the run were 10 levels higher, and they never come up empty. Bounty and quest gear rolls the same table at your level, read further down for the better rewards (like a Rare monster or a Champion), and is never empty. The forge makes exactly the piece you choose, at any level up to your Blacksmithing level. Selling Brenna Ironhand and The Collector buy gear, ingredients, modifiers and orbs at one shared price table. Brenna's menu has [Sell what you hold] and [Sell all gear] . See coins and bank . See also Rarity · Weapons · Armour · Sets · Amulets Orbs , to change the affixes on a piece The forge , to make your own"}, {"t": "Rarity", "u": "rarity.html", "c": "Gear & forge", "s": "The seven rarities from Common to God's Tool - colours, sockets, affixes, how much stronger each step is, and the drop odds by monster level.", "h": "The seven rarities Unique and Set are types, not rarities Sockets and affixes The extra base line (Rare and up) How much stronger is each step? Names Drop odds by monster level Raising rarity yourself See also", "x": "The seven rarities Every piece of gear has one of seven rarities. Rarity sets the name colour, how many sockets the piece can hold, how many affixes it rolls with, and how strong its base numbers are. # Rarity Colour Max sockets 1 Common white 1 2 Uncommon light blue 2 3 Rare blue 3 4 Mythic purple 4 5 Legendary orange 5 6 Extraordinary red 6 7 God's Tool gold 7 The same weapon base as Common, Uncommon and Rare. Mythic and higher use the Rare look with an enchanted glint. Dropped items show a beam in their rarity's colour, so you can spot good loot from a distance. Unique and Set are types, not rarities Hand-made items, such as set pieces , Legendary weapons and other named uniques, keep their own fixed rarity. A set can be Rare, Mythic or Legendary. A unique can be anything from Uncommon to God's Tool. Their rarity cannot be changed. Sockets and affixes Affixes are stat bonuses (\"+3 Might\") that sit in sockets . A filled socket shows ◆ and an open one shows ◇. A piece can never hold more sockets than its rarity allows. What a rolled drop comes with: Rarity Sockets Affixes Common 0-1 0 Uncommon 1-2 1-2 Rare 2-3 2-3 Mythic 3-4 3 Legendary 4-5 3-4 Extraordinary 5-6 4-5 (plus a skill socket) God's Tool 6-7 5-6 (plus a skill socket) Common and Uncommon pieces roll affixes from their own pool: weapon affixes on weapons, armour affixes on armour. Rare and up can roll any combat stat on any piece. Weapons never roll Vitality. Shields, armour and amulets can. Affixes lean toward the class a piece was made for. A stat the class likes tends to roll high, and a stat it doesn't like tends to roll low. Nothing is ruled out. Affix ranges grow with item level: both ends go up by 1 for every 10 item levels. Mythic and higher add +1 more to the top of the range for each step above Rare. Affix word Stat Range at item level 1 Rolls on Heavy Might 1-3 weapons Quick Ferocity 1-4 weapons Keen Precision 2-6 weapons Sturdy Fortitude 1-3 armour Plated Toughness 1-3 armour Fleet Swiftness 1-3 armour Rooted Steadfast 1-4 armour Hardy Vitality 1-4 anything but weapons Lucky Fortune 1-3 anything Mystic Arcana 1-3 anything The extra base line (Rare and up) Rare and better rolled pieces may carry one base line they normally wouldn't have. A weapon can get armour or toughness , and armour or a shield can get attack damage or attack speed . The chance grows with rarity: Rare Mythic Legendary Extraordinary God's Tool 25% 40% 55% 70% 85% How much stronger is each step? A piece's base numbers come from its power , which is its item level plus its rarity number (Common 1 up to God's Tool 7). On top of that, each rarity rolls inside its own quality window : Rarity Quality Common 85-100% Uncommon 90-105% Rare 95-110% Mythic 100-115% Legendary 105-120% Extraordinary 110-125% God's Tool 115-130% So a higher rarity is better at every level, but item level still matters most . A fresh Common at your level can beat an old Legendary from twenty levels ago. Worked examples are on the weapons page. Names Rarity Name shape Example Common the base name Levy Blade Uncommon affix word + base name Heavy Levy Blade Rare two random words (the base name moves to the second line) Velvet Debt Mythic and up the Rare name + an epithet Velvet Debt, the Last Favour The random words fit the item and its class. Staves get mage-like names, daggers get rogue-like ones, and so on. Drop odds by monster level Every monster gear drop rolls one row of this table, by the monster's level. The odds change every 5 levels: levels 1-4 use the level 1 row, 5-9 the level 5 row, and so on. Figures are % per gear drop. Monster level Common Uncommon Rare Mythic Legendary Extraordinary God's Tool nothing 1 40 15 7 3 2 1 0.5 31.5 5 35 20 8 3 2 1 0.7 30.3 10 31.25 21.25 9 4 2.75 1.5 0.77 29.5 15 27.5 22.5 10 5 3.5 2 0.85 28.7 20 23.75 23.75 11 6 4.25 2.5 0.92 27.8 25 20 25 12 7 5 3 1 27 30 18 25 13 7.8 5.6 3.4 1.1 26.1 35 16 25 14 8.6 6.2 3.8 1.2 25.2 40 14 25 15 9.4 6.8 4.2 1.3 24.3 45 12 25 16 10.2 7.4 4.6 1.4 23.4 50 10 25 17 11 8 5 1.5 22.5 Tougher monsters read further down the table: Magic monsters as if 3 levels higher, Rare +6, Champions +10 and bosses +15. Bosses never roll \"nothing\". The God's Tool column gives you one of the God's Tool weapons made for your class (see relics ). The other columns give a rolled piece of that rarity. Before any of this, the monster has to drop gear at all. See the drop chances on the gear page. Raising rarity yourself Orbs : an Orb of Transmutation turns a Common into an Uncommon, and a Regal Orb turns an Uncommon into a Rare. The forge : pieces come out at your Blacksmithing mastery's rarity, up to Legendary. A Star Shard pushes a piece one rarity higher. Extraordinary and God's Tool can't be crafted. They only drop. See also Gear · Orbs · Forge · Relics"}, {"t": "Weapons", "u": "weapons.html", "c": "Gear & forge", "s": "Every weapon type, which class uses it, how weapon damage scales with level and rarity, and the full list of rolled weapon bases.", "h": "Weapon types and classes How weapon damage works Rolled weapon bases Named weapons Skill sockets See also", "x": "Weapon types and classes Each class casts its abilities through its own weapon types. Anyone can swing any weapon, but only the right class can cast with it. Type Class Notes Sword Warrior Balanced damage and speed Axe Warrior About 30% more per hit than a sword, slower Mace Warrior About 20% more per hit than a sword, slower Shield Warrior Off hand. Gives armour instead of damage Dagger Rogue About 25% less per hit than a sword, much faster Bow Archer No melee damage. Its Draw Power adds % arrow damage Staff Mage Kept weak in melee on purpose. A Mage's damage is in the spells Mage Blade Mage A light sword that casts like a staff. Hits for about 75% of a sword Standard Herald The Herald's banner pole. Low melee, casts the Herald's hymns Weapons and shields always belong to their own type's class (a bow is always an Archer's), so they carry no \"Made for\" line. Only armour is rolled for a class. How weapon damage works Damage, attack speed and Draw Power are rolled when the weapon is made. They come from: Power = item level + rarity number (Common 1 … God's Tool 7). The type's base and growth per power point. A quality roll inside the rarity's window (Common 85-100% up to God's Tool 115-130%). See rarity . The base's own damage %. Heavy bases hit harder and swing slower, and light ones the other way round. Type Starts at Grows per power point Attack speed Sword 5 +0.20 about 1.6 Axe 6.5 +0.26 about 0.9 Mace 6 +0.24 about 0.9 Dagger 3.8 +0.13 about 2.4 Mage Blade 4 +0.15 about 1.7 Staff 2 +0.03 fist speed Standard 3 +0.06 fist speed Bow - +2% arrow damage (Draw Power) - Attack speed only moves a little: the type's speed ±5% at random, plus a tiny amount per power point. Your Ferocity is the real attack-speed stat. A plain sword's damage (low-high of the quality window, before the base's own %): Item level Common Uncommon Rare Mythic Legendary Extraordinary God's Tool 1 4.6-5.4 5.0-5.9 5.5-6.4 6.0-6.9 6.5-7.4 7.0-8.0 7.6-8.6 10 6.1-7.2 6.7-7.8 7.2-8.4 7.8-9.0 8.4-9.6 9.0-10.2 9.7-10.9 25 8.7-10.2 9.4-10.9 10.1-11.7 10.8-12.4 11.6-13.2 12.3-14.0 13.1-14.8 50 12.9-15.2 13.9-16.2 14.8-17.2 15.8-18.2 16.8-19.2 17.8-20.3 18.9-21.3 Your stats, perks and tree add to this. Might, for example, adds melee damage on top. See stats . Below the weapon's item level it does nothing. No damage and no casting until your character level catches up (see gear ). Rolled weapon bases Each base has its own name, damage % and speed %, and a level band : it only drops once your character reaches that level. Later bases are slightly better at the same item level, about 1% per 5 band levels. Normal, Magic and Rare versions each have their own look, and the Rare look of many bases is animated. The original bases (Warblade, Cleaver, Templar Maul, Kite Shield, Shiv, Hunter's Bow, Arcane Staff, Assassin's Dagger, Mage Blade, Herald's Standard) still drop at every level. Class Base Type Band Damage % Speed % Warrior Levy Blade Sword 1 96 104 Warrior Bog Chopper Axe 4 106 95 Warrior Bell Hammer Mace 8 104 98 Warrior Exile Greatsword Sword 12 124 83 Warrior Tower Shield Shield 12 112 (armour) - Warrior Dane Axe Axe 16 118 88 Warrior Ram's Head Mace 20 112 93 Warrior Oathblade Sword 25 103 102 Warrior Headsman's Axe Axe 30 128 83 Warrior Grave Mattock Mace 35 130 82 Warrior Barrow Door Shield 35 120 (armour) - Warrior Emberwrought Blade Sword 40 110 98 Mage Hedge Staff Staff 1 100 - Mage Mirelight Staff Staff 4 101 - Mage Inkblade Mage Blade 8 98 104 Mage Bone Wand Staff 12 102 - Mage Sigil Sabre Mage Blade 16 106 98 Mage Seer's Crook Staff 20 104 - Mage Tower Sabre Mage Blade 25 100 105 Mage Astral Spire Staff 30 106 - Mage Quillsword Mage Blade 35 97 110 Mage Ember Sceptre Staff 40 108 - Rogue Gutter Knife Dagger 1 95 105 Rogue Reed Knife Dagger 4 100 101 Rogue Cinquedea Dagger 8 112 91 Rogue Bodkin Dagger 12 92 112 Rogue Fang Knife Dagger 16 104 100 Rogue Ledger Knife Dagger 20 99 106 Rogue Whisper Blade Dagger 25 108 98 Rogue Duskfang Dagger 30 95 112 Rogue Twin Fangs Dagger 35 116 92 Rogue Throatcutter Dagger 40 112 96 Archer Sapling Bow Bow 1 100 - Archer Reed Bow Bow 4 101 - Archer Horn Bow Bow 8 102 - Archer Composite Bow Bow 12 102 - Archer Heron Bow Bow 16 103 - Archer Siege Bow Bow 20 104 - Archer Moonbow Bow 25 105 - Archer Thornbow Bow 30 106 - Archer Stormstring Bow 35 107 - Archer Elder Yew Bow 40 108 - Herald Camp Banner Standard 1 100 - Herald Road Pennant Standard 4 101 - Herald Bell Pole Standard 8 102 - Herald Choir Standard Standard 12 102 - Herald Lantern Pole Standard 16 103 - Herald Vesper Banner Standard 20 104 - Herald Wayfarer's Ensign Standard 25 105 - Herald Clarion Staff Standard 30 106 - Herald Sunstandard Standard 35 107 - Herald Exile's Colours Standard 40 108 - For bows, \"damage %\" is the base's Draw Power %. For shields it is the base's armour %. Named weapons Beyond rolled bases there are hand-made weapons with fixed stats: Uniques that drop from monsters: Emberbrand (active Ember Burst : a ring of fire burns enemies within 4 blocks), Warcaller ( War Cry : you and nearby allies gain Strength and Speed for 8s), Dawnhammer ( Holy Smite : light strikes the first enemy you look at, up to 12 blocks), Ghostfang ( Phantom Lunge : lunge forward and thrust a ghost blade about 6 blocks), Bulwark of Dawn (a shield) and The Rubber Chicken. Right-click to use an item's active skill. Signature weapons drop from bosses. Each has its own ability, which takes your first ability key while you hold the weapon: Weapon Type Signature Clanbreaker Axe A shockwave throws enemies within 5 blocks into the air and sunders their armour (-6 armour for 6s). Mycelial Greatbow Bow Charge for 1.5s, then loose 5 arrows at once, each dealing 50% of your arrow damage. Broodfang Dagger Slash the enemy in front of you and mark it with poison for 8s. The mark pulses, poisoning enemies within 3 blocks. Necrostaff Staff Raise 2 undead servants that fight for you for 20s (up to 4 at once). Defiling Staff Staff Plant a corpse mine where you look (up to 3). With 3 planted, right-click detonates them all. Blood Staff Staff For 2s, drain every hostile within 8 blocks. Each pulse that hits heals you. Legendary weapons : 25 class-locked weapons with big fixed stats. Extraordinary and God's Tool weapons : 15 class-locked weapons, each with its own passive. Named weapons also get an item level when they drop, and their numbers follow that level like everything else. Skill sockets A weapon with a skill socket can hold a skill gem , which then becomes your first ability while you hold it. Extraordinary and God's Tool weapons always have one. On other pieces you can add one at the forge with a Skill Socket Stone. See also Gear · Rarity · Armour · Forge · Abilities"}, {"t": "Armour", "u": "armour.html", "c": "Gear & forge", "s": "Helmets, chest pieces, leggings, boots and shields - how armour is rolled for each class, how much it gives, and how to wear 3D headpieces.", "h": "The armour slots Made for a class How much armour? Extra lines on Rare and up Named armour 3D headpieces See also", "x": "The armour slots Slot Type on the tooltip Share of a full set's armour Head Helmet 15% Chest Body Armour 40% Legs Leggings 30% Feet Boots 15% Off hand Shield (Warriors) its own value Armour only counts in its own slot. Like all gear, it has an item level: below that level it gives no armour, no toughness and no stats , and it doesn't count toward a set (see gear ). Made for a class When armour drops, it is rolled for a class : 70% of the time the receiver's class, otherwise one of the others. At the forge you choose the class yourself. The tooltip shows it as Made for ⚔ Warrior (plus the subclass when it was rolled for one). The class decides three things: The look. Warrior pieces are iron and chainmail. The other classes wear leather dyed in their colour, and Herald pieces carry his own trims. How heavy it is. Each class gets a share of the plain armour value: Made for Armour Warrior 130% Herald 95% Archer 85% Rogue 80% Mage 70% Which affixes it leans to. Warrior pieces tend to roll big defensive stats (Fortitude, Toughness, Vitality, Steadfast). Rogue and Archer pieces lean to Ferocity, Precision and Swiftness, and Mage and Herald pieces to Arcana. Any piece can still roll any stat. A favoured stat just tends to roll high, and an unfavoured one low. Only Warrior pieces roll armour toughness. Anyone can wear any armour, whatever it was made for. How much armour? A full four-piece set gives 2.5 + 0.1 armour per power point (item level + rarity number). That is then multiplied by the class share above and the piece's quality roll (see rarity ). Approximate armour from a full four-piece set (Common / Legendary, low-high): Item level Warrior Herald Archer Rogue Mage 1 3.0-3.5 / 4.2-4.8 2.2-2.6 / 3.1-3.5 2.0-2.3 / 2.8-3.2 1.8-2.2 / 2.6-3.0 1.6-1.9 / 2.3-2.6 10 4.0-4.7 / 5.5-6.2 2.9-3.4 / 4.0-4.6 2.6-3.1 / 3.6-4.1 2.4-2.9 / 3.4-3.8 2.1-2.5 / 2.9-3.4 25 5.6-6.6 / 7.5-8.6 4.1-4.8 / 5.5-6.3 3.7-4.3 / 4.9-5.6 3.5-4.1 / 4.6-5.3 3.0-3.6 / 4.0-4.6 50 8.4-9.9 / 10.9-12.5 6.1-7.2 / 8.0-9.1 5.5-6.5 / 7.1-8.2 5.2-6.1 / 6.7-7.7 4.5-5.3 / 5.9-6.7 Each piece is rounded on its own, so real sets can be a little off these numbers. Fortitude and Toughness from your stats add more armour on top. Shields give 0.5 + 0.03 armour per power point (about 0.5 at level 1 and 2 at level 50), times the base's own armour %. The Tower Shield is 112% and the Barrow Door 120%. Extra lines on Rare and up Rare and better rolled armour may carry one weapon line it normally wouldn't have: attack damage or attack speed . Rogue and Archer pieces lean toward these. See rarity for the chances. Named armour Set pieces give set bonuses when you wear several pieces of the same set. Uniques have fixed stats and often a passive skill, such as Owleye Cowl ( Night Eyes : you can see in the dark), Springheel Boots ( Spring Step : you jump much higher) or Salamander Mail ( Ember Ward : fire and lava can't burn you). Their armour follows their item level too. The value written for them is what they have at item level 25. Below that they have less, and above it more. 3D headpieces Set and unique helmets are 3D models worn on your head. They are not vanilla armour items, so right-click with one in your hand to put it on . Whatever you wore before comes into your hand. To take one off, click it out of your helmet slot. Their armour, toughness, stats, passives and set count work like any other helmet. Rolled helmets stay normal armour. See also Gear · Rarity · Sets · Amulets · Forge"}, {"t": "Sets", "u": "sets.html", "c": "Gear & forge", "s": "Every armour set - its pieces, its rarity, and the bonuses for wearing 2, 3 or 4 pieces, including the set ultimates.", "h": "How sets work Mixing sets Stag of Varr Class sets Subclass sets Sets for any class Only one ultimate at a time See also", "x": "How sets work A set piece belongs to a named set. Wear several pieces of the same set and you get set bonuses : Pieces count only when worn in their own slot . An amulet that belongs to a set counts from an amulet slot. Bonuses stack . With 4 pieces on you also get the 2-piece and 3-piece bonuses. A piece below its item level doesn't count (see gear ). Stat bonuses are bonus levels on your stats , shown in aqua on the sheet. Set ultimates: the big class sets give a new ultimate ability at 4 pieces, if your class (or subclass) matches the set. It joins your ability pool and takes the ultimate's key by default. You can move it like any other ability (see abilities ). Take a piece off and the key falls back to its normal ability. Put it back on and the set ultimate returns. Every set piece tooltip lists the set's bonuses and, on class sets, the set ultimate. Set and unique helmets are 3D headpieces: right-click with one in your hand to wear it (see armour ). Mixing sets These smaller sets drop from monsters like other named items (see gear ). They are Rare . Set Pieces 2 worn 3 worn 4 worn Dawnguard Dawnguard Helm, Plate, Greaves, Sabatons +4 Vitality - +3 Fortitude and Dawnlight : slow regeneration Nightstalker Nightstalker Hood, Jerkin, Boots (3 pieces) +3 Swiftness Shadow Veil : invisible while sneaking - Wildwood Wildwood Hood, Jerkin, Leggings, Boots +4 Precision - +3 Swiftness and Hawkeye : while sneaking, enemies within 24 blocks glow Clanblood Clanblood Helm, Plate, Greaves, Sabatons +4 Might - +3 Toughness and Bloodrage : below 5 hearts Strength; below 3 hearts Strength II and 2 hearts of Absorption for 6s (20s cooldown) Tidecaller Tidecaller Sou'wester, Oilskin, Waders, Sea Boots +4 Arcana - Tideborn : Water Breathing and Dolphin's Grace; Regeneration while in water Stag of Varr A Rare four-piece set (Helm, Plate, Greaves, Sabatons) earned as a reward from a quest line, not from monsters. 2 worn 4 worn +4 Vitality Hold the Ford : every hostile within 6 blocks is a stack (max 5), each giving +1 armour and +4% melee damage. At 5 stacks you can't be knocked back. The stacks fall away 3s after the last hostile is gone. Class sets Four pieces each (a crown, hood, helm or similar, a chest piece, leggings and boots). The set ultimate at 4 pieces works only for the class named. These sets are Mythic . Set For 2 worn 3 worn 4 worn: set ultimate Archon Mage +5 Arcana +4 Vitality, +3 Precision Arcane Cataclysm : three rings of arcane blasts roll out from you. Each enemy hit refunds mana. Scales with Arcana. Stormhawk Archer +5 Precision +4 Swiftness, +3 Fortune Rain of Arrows : spirit arrows rain on the spot you look at for 4s. Scales with Precision. Shadowreaper Rogue +5 Ferocity +4 Swiftness, +3 Precision Thousand Cuts : blink between up to 6 enemies within 10 blocks, slashing each. Scales with Ferocity and Precision. Warlord Warrior +5 Might +4 Toughness, +3 Vitality Avatar of War : 8s of more attack damage and armour, no knockback, and melee hits send a shockwave. Scales with Might. Subclass sets These Legendary sets give their ultimate only to the subclass named (see classes ). Set For 2 worn 3 worn 4 worn: set ultimate Cindermantle Pyromancer (Mage) +5 Arcana +4 Might, +3 Vitality Inferno : 6s in which everything within 5 blocks burns and takes damage twice a second. Scales with Arcana. Glacier Frostbinder (Mage) +5 Arcana +4 Fortitude, +3 Steadfast Absolute Zero : freeze every enemy within 8 blocks for 4s, then shatter them. Scales with Arcana. Deadeye Sniper (Archer) +5 Precision +4 Fortune, +3 Steadfast Deadeye Focus : for 8s every arrow you land strikes a second time. Scales with Precision. Galeweave Windrunner (Archer) +5 Swiftness +4 Precision, +3 Vitality Cyclone : a 4s tornado that drags enemies in and lifts them. Scales with Precision and Swiftness. Veilshroud Assassin (Rogue) +5 Ferocity +4 Precision, +3 Swiftness Assassinate : blink behind your target and strike. Below 30% health it dies (not bosses). Scales with Ferocity and Precision. Plaguebearer Poisoner (Rogue) +5 Precision +4 Fortune, +3 Vitality Pandemic : every enemy within 10 blocks falls sick for 8s, and it spreads. Scales with Precision and Fortune. Duelist's Finery Duelist (Rogue) +5 Ferocity +4 Swiftness, +3 Fortitude En Garde : a 6s stance in which you are faster and better armoured, and melee hits deal bonus damage. Scales with Ferocity and Precision. Sanctified Bulwark Paladin (Warrior) +5 Fortitude +4 Vitality, +3 Might Divine Aegis : for 8s you and nearby allies heal, and whatever hits you is smitten. Scales with Might and Vitality. Bloodhowl Berserker (Warrior) +5 Might +4 Ferocity, +3 Vitality Blood Frenzy : 10s of more attack damage and speed, and melee hits heal you. Scales with Might and Ferocity. Sets for any class Set Rarity 2 worn 3 worn 4 worn Wayfarer Mythic +2 to every combat stat (Might, Ferocity, Precision, Arcana, Vitality, Fortitude, Toughness, Swiftness, Steadfast, Fortune) another +2 to each Second Wind : below 3 hearts you go back to half health and get Regeneration, once a minute Rubber Chicken Knight Mythic +5 Fortune +4 Vitality, +3 Swiftness Egg Barrage (set ultimate, any class): lob 20 eggs. Each one that hits bursts and hatches an angry chicken. Scales with Fortune. The class, subclass and any-class sets (Archon through Rubber Chicken Knight) are not part of the ordinary monster loot. Only one ultimate at a time You can only have one ultimate on your keys at a time: your class ultimate or a worn set's. See abilities . See also Armour · Rarity · Abilities · Stats"}, {"t": "Legendary Weapons", "u": "legendary-weapons.html", "c": "Gear & forge", "s": "The 25 class-locked Legendary weapons, five per class - their stats, where they come from and how the class lock works.", "h": "What they are Class-locked Where they come from The 25 Warrior Mage Rogue Archer Herald See also", "x": "What they are Legendary weapons are 25 hand-made weapons, five for each class . Each one is a Legendary-rarity unique with high fixed damage and three big stat bonuses , about twice what a signature weapon gives. They have no special powers. Their strength is in their numbers. Every one has its own animated look. Don't confuse them with ordinary Legendary-rarity rolled gear (orange names with random words). These are the named weapons listed below. Class-locked Only its own class can wield a Legendary weapon. The tooltip says so: ✦ Legendary · only a Mage can wield it . In the hands of any other class it does nothing: no damage, no stats, no casting . A chat line tells you, for example, \"Aeonspire answers only to a Mage. In your hands it deals no damage and casts nothing.\" Change class and it is checked again at once. Like all gear, a Legendary weapon also has an item level and only works from that character level up (see gear ). Where they come from They never come from the ordinary loot tables. A Legendary weapon is always one of the receiver's class's five. A character with no class can't get one. Source Chance A Rare monster 1 in 1,000 A Champion 1 in 200 Some of the great bosses 1 in 40 When you find one, only you are told: \"✦ You found a Legendary weapon: Kingsbane\" , with a big title on screen. The 25 Damage and speed are the values at item level 25 . The weapon you get follows its own item level: lower below 25, higher above. Bows have no melee damage. Like every bow, they get Draw Power (+% arrow damage) for their item level and rarity. Warrior Weapon Type Damage / speed Bonuses Look Kingsbane Sword 12 / 1.2 +7 Might, +6 Toughness, +4 Vitality Black steel under a golden crown. Worldsplitter Axe 14 / 0.8 +7 Might, +5 Fortitude, +5 Vitality Obsidian with fire still moving inside. Thunderhowl Mace 13 / 0.85 +6 Might, +6 Steadfast, +5 Toughness It hums before it lands. The Iron Vow Sword 10 / 1.5 +7 Fortitude, +6 Vitality, +4 Steadfast White steel and gold wings. Rimefang Sword 11 / 1.35 +7 Toughness, +6 Vitality, +4 Might A blade of ice that does not melt. Mage Weapon Type Damage / speed Bonuses Look Aeonspire Staff 4 +8 Arcana, +5 Vitality, +4 Precision A night sky held on a rod. The Burning Treatise Staff 4 +8 Arcana, +5 Might, +4 Fortitude A sceptre crowned in fire. Voidglass Requiem Mage Blade 7 / 1.7 +7 Arcana, +5 Precision, +4 Swiftness A blade of black-violet glass. Moonwrit Mage Blade 7 / 1.75 +7 Arcana, +5 Vitality, +4 Steadfast A curved blade the colour of moonlight. Verdant Heart Staff 4 +7 Arcana, +6 Vitality, +4 Fortune A staff that is still alive. Rogue Weapon Type Damage / speed Bonuses Look Nightsong Dagger 7 / 2.4 +7 Ferocity, +6 Swiftness, +4 Precision A wavy blade with stars in the steel. Widowmaker Dagger 7 / 2.4 +7 Ferocity, +6 Precision, +4 Fortune A hooked fang that never stops dripping. The Ninefold Fang Dagger 8 / 2.3 +7 Precision, +5 Ferocity, +5 Swiftness Two bone blades, bound in gold. Ashwhisper Dagger 7 / 2.4 +7 Swiftness, +5 Ferocity, +5 Fortune Grey ash with a burning edge. Gilded Debt Dagger 6 / 2.6 +8 Fortune, +5 Precision, +4 Swiftness A golden needle with a coin on a chain. Archer Weapon Type Bonuses Look Skyrender Bow +8 Precision, +5 Swiftness, +4 Ferocity A storm caught between two horns. Sunshot Bow +7 Precision, +6 Ferocity, +4 Vitality A golden recurve that is warm to touch. The Long Patience Bow +7 Precision, +5 Steadfast, +5 Fortitude Ancient yew with moss in its joints. Wyrmstring Bow +7 Precision, +5 Ferocity, +5 Swiftness Red scale and bone, strung with fire. Whisperwind Bow +7 Swiftness, +6 Precision, +4 Fortune Pale wood that weighs nothing. Herald Weapon Type Damage Bonuses Look Banner of a Hundred Fires Standard 4 +7 Arcana, +6 Vitality, +4 Fortune Crimson cloth on a golden glaive. Choir Eternal Standard 4 +8 Vitality, +5 Arcana, +4 Steadfast A sun on a silver pole, and a long white banner. The Unbowed Standard 4 +6 Fortitude, +6 Vitality, +5 Arcana Black iron and a teal banner that never falls. Lantern of Lost Roads Standard 4 +7 Arcana, +6 Fortune, +4 Vitality A blue lantern that shows the way home. The Ashen Oriflamme Standard 4 +6 Arcana, +6 Vitality, +5 Fortune A long scarlet banner on a burning spear. See also Extraordinary and God's Tool weapons · Weapons · Rarity · Classes"}, {"t": "Extraordinary & God's Tool Weapons", "u": "relics.html", "c": "Gear & forge", "s": "The 15 relic weapons - 10 Extraordinary and 5 God's Tools, three per class - with their stats, their unique passives and where they come from.", "h": "What they are Class-locked Passive damage The 15 Where they come from See also", "x": "What they are These are the strongest weapons in the game, often just called relics . There are 15, three per class: 10 Extraordinary weapons (red), two per class. 5 God's Tools (gold), one per class. Each has bigger fixed numbers than a Legendary weapon , four stat bonuses , and one passive of its own, shown on the tooltip. Every relic always has a skill socket for a skill gem . Each one is drawn from scratch and animated. Class-locked Like the Legendaries, a relic only answers to its own class. In anyone else's hands it deals no damage, gives no stats and its passive stays silent. It also has an item level and only works from that character level up (see gear ). Relics can't be changed. Orbs of Sockets, Orbs of Affixing and the forge all refuse them. Passive damage Passive damage grows with your character level . The base is 3 + 0.15 per level (7.5 at level 30), and each passive deals its own share of that. Starfall, for example, hits for 150% of the base, about 11 at level 30. It's spell damage, so it never triggers another on-hit passive. The 15 Damage and speed are the values at item level 25 . The weapon you get follows its own item level. Bows get Draw Power like every bow. Class Tier Weapon Type Damage / speed Bonuses Passive Warrior Extraordinary Faultline Sword 15 / 1.1 +8 Might, +7 Toughness, +5 Vitality, +4 Steadfast Earthbreak : melee hits have a 1 in 4 chance to crack the ground with magma. Every monster within 3 blocks of the target is hurt, staggered and scorched. Warrior Extraordinary The Drowned Anchor Mace 15 / 0.85 +8 Fortitude, +7 Toughness, +5 Vitality, +4 Might Hold Fast : below half health you get Resistance. Once every 15 seconds you also get 2 hearts of Absorption, and the anchor drags monsters within 4 blocks down. Warrior God's Tool Eclipse Axe 18 / 0.85 +10 Might, +8 Fortitude, +6 Toughness, +5 Vitality Totality : every 5th melee hit the light goes out. Everything in a line 6 blocks ahead is struck hard, thrown up and marked by the dark. Mage Extraordinary The Orrery Staff 5 +9 Arcana, +6 Vitality, +5 Precision, +4 Fortitude Starfall : every 4 seconds a star falls on a monster within 12 blocks, stunning it for a moment. Mage Extraordinary Coldsong Mage Blade 9 / 1.6 +8 Arcana, +6 Precision, +5 Swiftness, +4 Vitality Shatter : melee hits have a 1 in 3 chance to freeze the target solid and throw ice at two more monsters nearby, chilling them. Mage God's Tool The First Page Staff 6 +11 Arcana, +7 Vitality, +6 Precision, +5 Steadfast The Three Words : three words orbit you. Every 2 seconds they strike every monster within 4 blocks and leave it dazed. Rogue Extraordinary Duskmoth Dagger 9 / 2.4 +9 Ferocity, +7 Swiftness, +5 Precision, +4 Fortune Nightfall : sneaking makes you invisible. The first hit out of it deals triple damage and blinds the target. Rogue Extraordinary Bloodbriar Dagger 9 / 2.4 +9 Ferocity, +7 Precision, +5 Vitality, +4 Fortune Bleed : hits make the target bleed, taking damage every second for 4 seconds while thorns hold it. Hitting again starts the bleed over. Rogue God's Tool The Final Hour Dagger 11 / 2.4 +11 Ferocity, +8 Precision, +6 Swiftness, +5 Fortune Midnight : hits on a monster below a quarter of its health end it. Bosses take half again instead. Archer Extraordinary Stormwing Bow - +9 Precision, +7 Swiftness, +5 Ferocity, +4 Vitality Tailwind : while held you have Speed and Jump Boost. Arrow hits have a 1 in 4 chance of a gust that hurts and lifts every monster within 3 blocks, and they drift down slowly. Archer Extraordinary Wyrmjaw Bow - +9 Precision, +7 Ferocity, +5 Might, +4 Vitality Dragonfire : arrow hits set the target alight. 1 in 5 bursts in dragonfire, burning and weakening every monster within 3 blocks. Archer God's Tool The Sunwheel Bow - +11 Precision, +8 Swiftness, +6 Ferocity, +5 Vitality Judgement : every 4th arrow hit calls a pillar of light down on the target and everything within 3 blocks, dazzling them. A chime tells you when the next hit will judge. Herald Extraordinary The Everbloom Standard 5 +9 Vitality, +7 Arcana, +5 Fortune, +4 Steadfast Spring's Breath : every 5 seconds, you and every player within 8 blocks get Regeneration. Herald Extraordinary The Stag King's Totem Standard 5 +8 Arcana, +7 Fortitude, +5 Vitality, +4 Might War Chant : while held, you and every player within 8 blocks have Strength. In a fight the war drums sound and nearby monsters are cowed. Herald God's Tool Phoenix Ascendant Standard 6 +11 Vitality, +8 Arcana, +6 Fortitude, +5 Fortune Rebirth : monsters within 4 blocks burn in its light every 2 seconds and catch fire. A player within 10 blocks who falls below 3 hearts is lifted to half health (each player once a minute). Archer class abilities that fire arrows also trigger a bow relic's on-hit passive. Where they come from A relic is always one of the receiver's class's weapons of that tier. A character with no class can't get one. The gear drop table: the God's Tool column of the rarity table (0.5% of gear drops at level 1, up to 1.5% at level 50) gives you your class's God's Tool . The Extraordinary column gives a rolled red piece of gear, not one of these weapons. Champions: 1 in 4,000 for an Extraordinary and 1 in 20,000 for a God's Tool, on top of their normal loot. Bosses: some of the great bosses have their own small chance. High-tier dungeon and raid clears , rolled for each player: Clear Extraordinary God's Tool Key tier 4 1 in 125 1 in 1,000 Key tier 5 (including Heroic raids) about 1 in 60 1 in 500 Tiers 1-3 have no relic roll. No duplicates: if a relic would drop that you already own (carried, in your ender chest, or on record as found), it rerolls into one of your class's that you don't have. A God's Tool you already own becomes an Extraordinary you don't. Only if you own them all does the duplicate drop. Relics are not bound to you. A relic flies to its receiver and can then be traded, and the class lock decides who can use it. Every Extraordinary or God's Tool weapon that drops is announced to everyone. That includes rolled Extraordinary weapons, and the finder gets a big title. See also Legendary weapons · Rarity · Gems · Weapons"}, {"t": "Orbs", "u": "orbs.html", "c": "Gear & forge", "s": "The crafting orbs - what Transmutation, Regal, Chaos and Scribing do, how to use them on a crafting table, where they drop, and their use on dungeon keys.", "h": "Using an orb The four orbs Where orbs come from Sockets and single affixes Orbs on dungeon keys Skill gems See also", "x": "Using an orb Orbs change the affixes and rarity of gear you already have. You use them on a crafting table , without any menu: Drop the item (Q) so it lands on top of a crafting table. Drop the orb on the same table, right next to the item. The orb works on the item within a moment and is used up. The item pops back up, rebuilt, for you to pick up. If the orb doesn't fit the item, a red line in chat tells you why and the orb is not spent. Pick both up and try again. Only a freshly dropped orb works (within about 3 seconds of landing), and one orb works per drop. An orb never changes a piece's item level or made-for class . It works on the affixes, sockets and rarity. When the rarity goes up, the base numbers rise to the new rarity, and the piece remembers where in the quality window it rolled, so an upgrade never makes it worse. Messages go only to the player who threw the orb. Each orb leaves its mark at the bottom of the tooltip: ◈ Transmutation, ✦ Regal, ☄ Chaos, ✎ Scribing. Chaos also counts how many times the piece was rerolled. Affixes need open sockets (◇). See rarity for how many sockets each rarity can hold. The four orbs Orb Works on What it does Orb of Transmutation a Common item with an open socket It becomes Uncommon with 1-2 affixes (as many as it has open sockets). A one-socket item gets a second, open socket. Regal Orb an Uncommon item with an open socket It becomes Rare . It keeps its affixes and adds one more. Chaos Orb a Rare item with affixes It rerolls every affix into a new set, with the same number of affixes. Orb of Scribing any item that can hold affixes It wipes every added affix. The sockets stay, now open. A set piece's or unique's own fixed stats stay too. New affixes follow the same rules as drops. They lean toward the piece's made-for class, Rare and up can roll any combat stat, and weapons never roll Vitality. Extraordinary and God's Tool gear can't be crafted on. Set pieces and uniques keep their own rarity, so of these four only Scribing works on them. Where orbs come from Monster Orb drop Normal 1% Magic 5% Rare 20% Champion always Mini-boss / boss always Which orb drops: Orb Share Orb of Transmutation 40% Chaos Orb 25% Regal Orb 20% Orb of Scribing 15% Brenna Ironhand and The Collector also buy orbs (see coins and bank ). Sockets and single affixes Adding sockets and single affixes is the forge 's job. Void Pearls and Shulker Plates give a forged piece extra sockets. Orbs of Sockets and Orbs of Affixing still exist and still work if you come across one, but they are not part of monster loot. If an orb tells you an item \"has no open socket\", that item can't take more affixes from orbs. Look for a drop with more open sockets, or forge one. Orbs on dungeon keys Two orbs also work on dungeon keys, in the same way on a crafting table: Regal Orb: the key goes up one tier (up to tier 5). Chaos Orb: rerolls the key's modifiers. Skill gems Skill gems are set into a weapon's skill socket the same way: drop the weapon on a crafting table, then drop the gem on it. See also Rarity · Gear · Forge · Gems"}, {"t": "Gems", "u": "gems.html", "c": "Gear & forge", "s": "Forge gems (Ruby to Topaz) and the stat each one rolls per tier, plus the six skill gems you can set into a weapon's skill socket.", "h": "Forge gems Where forge gems come from Skill gems Skill sockets Setting a gem Casting Where skill gems come from See also", "x": "Exile RPG has two kinds of gems: Forge gems (Ruby, Jade, Moonstone...) go into a piece at the forge and become its stat affixes. Skill gems (Starfall Volley, Riftblade...) go into a weapon's skill socket and give it an ability. Forge gems There is one gem for each combat stat, in three tiers (✦, ✦✦, ✦✦✦). Put a gem into a piece at the forge and it becomes an affix of its stat . Its tier decides the range the affix rolls in: Gem Stat ✦ ✦✦ ✦✦✦ Ruby Might +1 to +3 +3 to +5 +5 to +8 Garnet Vitality +1 to +4 +4 to +7 +7 to +10 Amber Ferocity +1 to +4 +4 to +7 +7 to +10 Jade Precision +2 to +6 +6 to +10 +10 to +15 Moonstone Arcana +1 to +3 +3 to +5 +5 to +8 Opal Fortitude +1 to +3 +3 to +5 +5 to +8 Onyx Toughness +1 to +3 +3 to +5 +5 to +8 Sapphire Swiftness +1 to +3 +3 to +5 +5 to +8 Aquamarine Steadfast +1 to +4 +4 to +7 +7 to +10 Topaz Fortune +1 to +3 +3 to +5 +5 to +8 How forge gems behave: Gems of one stat add up into one affix line. Three ✦✦ Rubies make one Might affix rolling +9 to +15. All of them share one affix slot. The metal rolls them. Every gem affix is rolled several times and the best roll stays. The count is the metal's rank (copper 1 up to netherite 5), plus 1 for each tier your ingredients average above ✦, plus bonuses from modifiers and perks. See forge . Gem affixes are fixed numbers . They don't grow with the piece's level. Garnet (Vitality) can't go into a weapon. Weapons never carry Vitality. The Blacksmithing passive Gem Setter makes every gem count one tier higher (✦✦✦ is the top). Where forge gems come from Mining. Each ore you mine has a 2.5% chance of a modifier from the ore list, and each stone-type block a 0.3% chance from the stone list. Gems are on both lists. The gem's tier follows your Mining level: ✦ from level 1, ✦✦ from 15, ✦✦✦ from 30, and one tier higher 5% of the time. The Mining passive Gem Sense makes these finds half again as common, and the Prospector Dust armour modifier adds more (see ingredients ). Brenna Ironhand sometimes stocks a ✦✦ gem among her daily materials. Combine: at the forge, 9 gems of one tier make 1 of the next tier (9 ✦ = 1 ✦✦, 9 ✦✦ = 1 ✦✦✦). Skill gems A skill gem is an ability you can put into a weapon. Each gem is made for one weapon type, but it fits any weapon with a skill socket and works the same in all of them. Skill sockets A skill socket is one empty slot on a weapon: Extraordinary and God's Tool weapons always have one, including every relic . At the forge , a Skill Socket Stone (any piece) or a Void Crown Fragment (helmets) adds one to the piece you make. Setting a gem Drop the weapon on a crafting table , then drop the gem on it, the same way you use an orb . A socket that already holds a gem refuses another. Casting While you hold the weapon, the gem takes your first ability key . On your own class's weapon you cast it like your first ability. On any other weapon (or with no class), right-click casts it, or sneak + draw on a bow. Each gem has its own mana cost and its own cooldown , separate from your class abilities. Its damage scales with the stats it lists, whatever your class or weapon. Gem Made for Mana Cooldown What it does Damage Starfall Volley Bow 45 20s Loose an arrow of light straight up. Two seconds later, twelve stars rain on a 5-block circle where you looked. 4 + 30% Precision per star Riftblade Sword 40 16s Dash 8 blocks straight through enemies, leaving a tear in the air that detonates along its whole length. 14 + 60% Might + 30% Ferocity Earthsplitter Axe 45 18s An overhead chop opens a fissure 12 blocks forward. Everything on the line is thrown into the air. 12 + 70% Might + 40% Fortitude Hundred Shadows Dagger 40 18s Blink between up to 5 enemies within 10 blocks, striking each once, then reappear where you started. 7 + 40% Ferocity + 30% Swiftness per strike Event Horizon Staff 55 22s A black orb opens where you look and drags enemies inward for 2.5 seconds, then collapses on them. 16 + 90% Arcana Sanctum Bell Mace 50 22s Slam the ground: a great bell rises and tolls three times. Each toll strikes enemies and heals players within 6 blocks. 8 + 40% Vitality + 30% Might per toll, plus a heal Where skill gems come from Skill gems drop from bosses. Bigger bosses give each player nearby their own chance at one. See also Forge · Ingredients · Relics · Abilities"}, {"t": "The Forge", "u": "forge.html", "c": "Gear & forge", "s": "Blacksmithing - opening the forge at an anvil, the patterns, mastery and metals, picking a level, affix slots, gem rolls, modifiers and the Blacksmithing perks.", "h": "Blacksmithing in short Opening the forge The forge screen, top to bottom Patterns The rules for putting things in Affix slots How the affixes are made Burning The forging show Training Blacksmithing Perks Passives (choose one per tier) Brenna Ironhand See also", "x": "Blacksmithing in short At the forge you make your own gear . You pick what to make and its level, put in metal, wood and hides, then add gems and modifiers to decide exactly which affixes it gets. There's nothing random on a forged piece apart from the gem rolls: it has only what you put in . Blacksmithing (⚒) is a stat on your character sheet next to the gathering stats. Its level is your mastery , and it decides: the rarity your pieces come out at, the best metal you're allowed to use, the highest level you can forge a piece at (your Blacksmithing level). Blacksmithing level Mastery Best metal Pieces come out 1-10 Common Copper Common 11-21 Uncommon Iron Uncommon 22-32 Rare Gold Rare 33-43 Mythic Diamond Mythic 44-50 Legendary Netherite Legendary So a crafted piece can be very strong for its level, but it stays at the level you forged it. A well-made level 5 sword can still be outclassed by a plain drop a few levels later, unless your modifiers carry it further. Opening the forge You need an anvil within 3 blocks (any anvil, chipped or damaged too). Then open the forge in any of these ways: Right-click the Exile's Journal near an anvil. To open the journal itself there, sneak. [Open the forge] on the Blacksmithing page of your character sheet. [⚒ Open the forge] in Brenna Ironhand's menu. Brenna counts as an anvil anywhere within 8 blocks of her. The forge is a chat menu. If you walk away from the anvil it closes (\"You stepped away from the anvil\"). Whatever you put in stays in the forge until you forge it or take it back, even if you close the menu. The forge screen, top to bottom The header: your Blacksmithing level and mastery (hover to see your pieces' rarity and best metal), and [✖ Close] . Workbench: [Combine 9 → next tier] . Hold 9 of a ✦ ingredient or gem to make one ✦✦, or 9 ✦✦ to make one ✦✦✦. With the Reclaimer perk you also get [Reclaim held piece] . ① Make: the patterns, grouped as Armour [Helmet] [Chest] [Legs] [Boots], Weapon [Sword] [Axe] [Mace] [Dagger] [Bow] [Staff] [Mage Blade] [Standard] and Shield [Shield]. Hover a pattern to see what it needs. The one you picked is yellow. ② For (armour only) : the class the armour is made for. [My class] is the default. Weapons and shields are always made for their own type's class, so this line doesn't appear for them. Level (② for weapons and shields, ③ for armour): [-10] [-1] level [+1] [+10] [Max]. This is the piece's item level, which decides its damage or armour and the character level that can use it. It goes from 1 up to your Blacksmithing level. It follows your Blacksmithing level as you train until you touch the row, and [Max] hands it back to following. The piece: \"▸ Sword · Iron Ingot · 3 in\" (the pattern, the metal and how many ingredients are in). Under it is the requirements line, e.g. Ingots 1/2 · Wood 0/1 · Modifiers 1/6 · Affix slots 1/3 , then the modifiers you've put in, then Gem affixes roll best of N . Hover these for details. Put in: [+ Add what I hold] moves the ingredient or modifier in your main hand into the piece. [↩ Take it all back] returns everything. [⚒ Forge it] , alone on the last line, once everything is in. Patterns Pattern Needs Helmet 5 ingots + 2 hides Chestplate 8 ingots + 4 hides Leggings 7 ingots + 3 hides Boots 4 ingots + 2 hides Sword 2 ingots + 1 wood Axe 3 ingots + 2 wood Mace 3 ingots + 2 wood Dagger 1 ingot + 1 hide Bow 3 wood + 2 hides Staff 1 ingot + 2 wood Mage Blade 1 ingot + 1 wood Standard 3 wood + 2 hides Shield 1 ingot + 6 wood You can only change the pattern while the forge is empty (\"Take it all back before changing the pattern\"). The piece's base is one of the made-for class's bases of that type, in that class's look. The rules for putting things in One metal per piece. Every ingot must be the same metal. The metal must be within your mastery. A Common smith can only use copper. An Ancient Sigil put in first lets any metal in. A family the pattern doesn't need, or already has enough of, is refused (\"The ingots are all in\"). Modifier slots: up to 6 modifiers go into one piece (7 with Legend Smith). Each modifier must fit the piece: Weapons ones go into anything held (melee weapons, bows, standards), Armour ones into armour and shields, Bows ones into bows, and so on. A Hide Scrap counts as one hide of any kind. Some modifiers are one per piece : Bone Dust, Echo Shard, Reverb Echo, Star Shard, Skill Socket Stone, Void Crown Fragment, Heart of the Fallen, Ancient Sigil, Amethyst Cluster and Shulker Plate. Weapons never carry Vitality , so a Garnet can't go into a weapon. Every ingredient and modifier is listed with what it does on the ingredients page. Affix slots The metal decides how many stat lines a piece can carry: Metal Affix slots Copper 2 Iron 3 Gold 4 Diamond 5 Netherite 6 An Amethyst Cluster and a Heart of the Fallen each add one more. One slot holds one stat line. That's either a gem's stat (more gems of the same stat share the slot) or a fixed-stat modifier such as Sinew or Hunters Eye. The wood and hide bonus is free and needs no slot. Until you add an ingot, your mastery's metal counts. The forge refuses a new stat when every slot is used, and it refuses an ingot whose metal has fewer slots than the piece already uses. How the affixes are made Gems become one affix per stat. Gems of the same stat add up, so three ✦✦ Rubies make one Might affix of +9 to +15. See gems for every gem's range. The metal rolls the gems. Each gem affix is rolled several times and the best roll stays. The number of tries is the metal's rank (copper 1, iron 2, gold 3, diamond 4, netherite 5), plus 1 for each tier your ingredients average above ✦, plus 3 for an Echo Shard. The menu shows the result as Gem affixes roll best of N . Wood and hide bonus: the wood you used most and the hide you used most each add their stat, in a range set by their average tier, rolled the same best-of way. Wood and hides list their ranges on their tooltips. Flat bonuses come after the roll: Coarse Grit +1 and Heart of the Fallen +2 to every gem affix, Shulker Plate -3, the Keen Edge passive +1 on weapons, and the Masters Mark perk +1. An affix never goes below 1. Bone Dust lifts your lowest gem affix to the top of its range. Reverb Echo makes your highest gem affix 50% bigger. Sockets come from the rarity, like a drop, plus a Void Pearl (+1) or Shulker Plate (+2). A skill socket only comes from a Skill Socket Stone or a Void Crown Fragment. Rarity is your mastery's. A Star Shard makes it one step higher (Legendary stays Legendary). Gem affixes are fixed numbers. They don't scale with the level you pick. The level only sets the piece's base damage or armour. Burning When you press [⚒ Forge it], each modifier has a 15% chance to burn away (\"Ruby burned away\"). The Careful Hands perk (Blacksmithing 10) stops that completely. The forging show The piece doesn't just appear. It floats over the anvil while each modifier sinks in, one at a time, and each gem line spins through its range before landing on its roll, e.g. \"The Might gems rolled +9 Might\" . The numbers it shows are the real ones. When it says \"It is done - the piece is on the anvil\" , the piece drops for you to pick up. A forged piece's tooltip says ⚒ Crafted · Iron ✦✦ · gem rolls best of 3 (with Masters Mark: Crafted by and your name). The stars are your ingredients' average tier. Training Blacksmithing You train Blacksmithing by forging. Each piece gives Blacksmithing XP for every ingredient that went in, more for better metals, and more for every modifier that went in. Some quests pay Blacksmithing XP too, and the Quest Board has forge quests that ask you to forge a certain kind of piece. Gathering is how you find the materials (see ingredients ). Perks Level Perk What it does 10 Careful Hands Modifiers never burn away in the forge. 20 Reclaimer [Reclaim held piece]: turn a piece you forged back into 1-3 of its ingots. 40 Masters Mark Your name on every piece you forge, and +1 to every gem affix. 50 Legend Smith A 7th modifier slot. Passives (choose one per tier) Tier Choices I (level 15) Keen Eye : ingredients drop 50% more often from gathering. · Salvager : modifiers drop 50% more often from monsters. · Apprentice : double Blacksmithing training XP. II (level 35) Spark Saver : patterns need one ingot less (never below 1). · Gem Setter : gems count one tier higher when forged in. · Keen Edge : forged weapons get +1 to every affix roll. The Trades nodes of your class skill tree help too: Forge Hand gives +1 gem roll on every piece, and Quick Kiln +25% Blacksmithing XP. Brenna Ironhand Brenna is the smith's NPC. She sells materials : six random rows a day of ingredients, modifiers and sometimes a ✦✦ gem, up to 5 of each. She counts as an anvil, has the [Combine 9] button too, and buys gear, ingredients, modifiers, gems and orbs. She doesn't upgrade or repair gear. That's what the orbs and the forge are for. See also Ingredients & modifiers · Gems · Rarity · Stats · Gear"}, {"t": "Ingredients & Modifiers", "u": "ingredients.html", "c": "Gear & forge", "s": "Every forge ingredient (ingots, heartwood, hides) and forge modifier - what each one does to a forged piece and where it drops.", "h": "How tiers work Ingots Heartwood Hides Gathering chances Gems Modifiers From Normal monsters From Magic monsters From Rare monsters From Champions and Reverbs From bosses Gathering-set modifiers Where modifiers drop See also", "x": "Everything that goes into the forge is either an ingredient (the metal, wood and hides a pattern needs) or a modifier (gems and other extras that shape the piece). Their tooltips list what they do. Ingredients and gems come in three tiers : ✦, ✦✦ and ✦✦✦. The gold pips sit in the top-left corner of the icon. How tiers work Higher-tier ingredients add gem rolls (the piece's ingredients' average tier: +1 try per tier above ✦) and a bigger wood and hide bonus. Combine: 9 of a ✦ ingredient or gem make 1 ✦✦, and 9 ✦✦ make 1 ✦✦✦. Use the [Combine] button at the forge or at Brenna. Most drops get their tier from your level in the stat that finds them (or the monster's level): ✦ from level 1, ✦✦ from 15, ✦✦✦ from 30 , and one tier higher 5% of the time. Mining and Logging work differently (below). Ingots Ingredient From Mastery Affix slots Gem rolls (best of) Copper Ingot copper ore Common 2 1 Iron Ingot iron ore Uncommon 3 2 Gold Ingot gold ore, nether gold ore Rare 4 3 Diamond diamond ore Mythic 5 4 Netherite Ingot ancient debris Legendary 6 5 Deepslate ores count too. An ingot's tooltip shows its gem rolls at its own tier: each tier above ✦ adds one. Heartwood Each wood adds one stat to the piece. The most-used wood in a piece gives its bonus (free, no affix slot), at a range set by the woods' average tier: Ingredient From Stat ✦ ✦✦ ✦✦✦ Oak Heartwood oak logs Arcana +1 to +2 +2 to +3 +3 to +4 Birch Heartwood birch and cherry logs Ferocity +1 to +2 +2 to +4 +4 to +5 Spruce Heartwood spruce logs Precision +1 to +3 +3 to +5 +5 to +8 Dark Oak Heartwood dark oak, mangrove logs, crimson stems Might +1 to +2 +2 to +3 +3 to +4 Jungle Heartwood jungle logs, warped stems Swiftness +1 to +2 +2 to +3 +3 to +4 Acacia Heartwood acacia logs Fortitude +1 to +2 +2 to +3 +3 to +4 Hides The most-used hide in a piece also gives its stat, the same way: Ingredient From Stat ✦ ✦✦ ✦✦✦ Leather Hide killing livestock (tier by your Farming level) Vitality +1 to +2 +2 to +4 +4 to +5 Scaled Hide fishing (tier by your Fishing level) Fortune +1 to +2 +2 to +3 +3 to +4 Chitin spiders and cave spiders, 15% (tier by their level) Toughness +1 to +2 +2 to +3 +3 to +4 Mire Hide monsters of the Drowned Barrow and Mirelings, 15% (tier by their level) Steadfast +1 to +2 +2 to +4 +4 to +5 Gathering chances Each gather (an ore, a log, a catch, a livestock kill) has a (your level ÷ 4)% chance of an ingredient: at least 1%, at most 12%. The Blacksmithing passive Keen Eye makes that 50% more often. Mining and Logging: that roll always gives a ✦ . On top of it, each block has its own 0.5% per level chance of a ✦✦, and separately of a ✦✦✦. A level 20 miner gets a 10% chance of each per ore. Fishing and livestock use the normal tier rule (✦ / ✦✦ from 15 / ✦✦✦ from 30). The Prospector Dust and Heartring armour modifiers (below) raise the find chances for Mining and Logging, and Brine Pearl raises Scaled Hide finds from fishing. Gems Ten gems, one per combat stat: Ruby (Might), Garnet (Vitality), Amber (Ferocity), Jade (Precision), Moonstone (Arcana), Opal (Fortitude), Onyx (Toughness), Sapphire (Swiftness), Aquamarine (Steadfast) and Topaz (Fortune). Each becomes an affix of its stat. Their ranges per tier are on the gems page. They come from mining (see below). Modifiers Up to 6 modifiers go into a piece (7 with Legend Smith). Each can burn away when you forge (15%) unless you have Careful Hands. Modifiers marked once can only go into a piece one at a time. Effects that work \"while held / worn\" act on the piece while it's in your hand or worn. The attribute ones don't stack across pieces. From Normal monsters Modifier Goes into Effect Bone Dust (once) any piece Its lowest gem affix goes to the top of its range. Sinew weapons and bows +2 Ferocity (attack speed, a faster draw). Tallow armour You burn for half as long. Coarse Grit any piece +1 to every gem affix. Hide Scrap any piece Counts as one hide of any kind in the pattern. From Magic monsters Modifier Goes into Effect Void Pearl any piece +1 socket. Blaze Heart weapons Hits set the target on fire for 3s. Frost Shard weapons Hits slow the target for 2s. Venom Gland weapons Hits poison the target for 3s. Phantom Weave boots and bows +3 Swiftness. Storm Quill bows Arrows fly 15% faster. Ember Glass armour Fire resistance for 3s when you're set alight (20s cooldown). Magic monsters also drop Bone Dust, Sinew and Tallow. From Rare monsters Modifier Goes into Effect Amethyst Cluster (once) any piece +1 affix slot. Shulker Plate (once) any piece +2 sockets, -3 to every gem affix. Ironbark Resin armour +3 Fortitude. Lifeblood Vial weapons Kills heal you 2 hearts. Hunters Eye bows +6 Precision. Echo Shard (once) any piece Every gem affix rolls 3 more times and keeps the best. Mirror Scale armour A monster that hits you takes 3 damage back. Quicksilver any piece Weapons: +4% attack speed. Armour: +2% move speed. Rare monsters also drop Void Pearl, Blaze Heart, Frost Shard, Venom Gland and Phantom Weave. From Champions and Reverbs Modifier Goes into Effect Star Shard (once) any piece It comes out one rarity above your mastery. Skill Socket Stone (once) any piece One skill socket for a skill gem . It's the only way to get one below Extraordinary. Titan Core armour +4 max health on top of its affixes. Dragon Scale armour +1 armour toughness on top of its affixes. Wraith Essence weapons Every 4th hit heals you 2 hearts. Gilded Thread any piece +3 Fortune. Reverb Echo (once) any piece Its highest gem affix echoes: +50%. Champions and Reverbs also drop Shulker Plate, Lifeblood Vial and Echo Shard. From bosses Modifier Goes into Effect Heart of the Fallen (once) any piece +2 to every gem affix and +1 affix slot. Ancient Sigil (once) any piece Needs no mastery for its metal. A Common smith can forge netherite with it. Void Crown Fragment (once) helmets +1 skill socket and +3 Arcana. Bosses also drop Star Shards and Skill Socket Stones. Gathering-set modifiers Three kinds of monster drop armour modifiers for gatherers: the Hollowed Delvers , the Fellkin and the Brinegill . Each kill has an 8% chance of one of its kind's four (12% with Salvager), with the tier set by the monster's level. These add up piece by piece across your armour. There's no set bonus. Modifier From ✦ / ✦✦ / ✦✦✦ Lamp Oil Hollowed Delvers +5 / 10 / 15% mining speed while you hold a pickaxe Vein Salt Hollowed Delvers +3 / 6 / 9% chance of an extra ore Prospector Dust Hollowed Delvers +10 / 15 / 20% ingredient, gem and modifier finds from mining Tally Chalk Hollowed Delvers +10 / 20 / 30% Mining XP Fellkin Sap the Fellkin +5 / 10 / 15% chopping speed while you hold an axe Twin Knot the Fellkin +3 / 6 / 9% double log chance Heartring the Fellkin +10 / 20 / 30% heartwood finds Bark Rune the Fellkin +10 / 20 / 30% Logging XP Bait Roe the Brinegill +3 / 6 / 9% double catch chance Brine Pearl the Brinegill +10 / 20 / 30% Scaled Hide finds from fishing Tide Scale the Brinegill +10 / 20 / 30% Fishing XP Snarled Line the Brinegill +2 / 4 / 6% chance of a treasure catch with every catch Where modifiers drop From monsters , on top of their normal loot. The modifier comes from the pool of the monster's rank (the tables above): Monster Chance of a modifier Normal 1.5% Magic 6% Rare 25% Champion / Reverb always, plus a 50% chance of a second one from the Rare pool Boss one from the boss pool, one Champion-pool and one Rare-pool modifier The Blacksmithing passive Salvager makes monster drops 50% more common. From mining: Each ore (including coal, redstone, lapis, emerald and quartz) has a 2.5% chance of one of: any gem, Ember Glass, Amethyst Cluster, Quicksilver or Gilded Thread. Each stone-type block has a 0.3% chance of one of: any gem, Coarse Grit, Amethyst Cluster or Echo Shard. The tier follows your Mining level. The Mining passive Gem Sense makes these finds half again as common, and Prospector Dust adds its %. Modifiers that work on affixes (the gems and the ones that change gem rolls, slots or stats) come up a little more often than the rest. Other sources: Brenna Ironhand sells six random material rows each day: ingredients, a Normal-pool and a Magic-pool modifier, and either a Rare-pool modifier or a ✦✦ mining find. Quests pay forge rewards. Kill quests pay a modifier from the pool their monsters drop from, gathering quests pay the ingredient of their trade, and crafting quests pay ingots or heartwood and Blacksmithing XP. Repeatable quests pay a random-tier ingredient and sometimes a modifier. See also The forge · Gems · Stats · Quests"}, {"t": "Amulets", "u": "amulets.html", "c": "Gear & forge", "s": "The three amulet slots, how amulets work, and every amulet with its stats and skill.", "h": "The amulet slots The amulets Where amulets come from See also", "x": "The amulet slots Amulets go in the three slots in the right-hand column of your main inventory (the last slot of each of the three inventory rows, not the hotbar). The resource pack draws a pendant outline in those slots, so you can see where they are. An amulet anywhere else in your bags does nothing. In an amulet slot, an amulet works like a worn piece of gear: Its stats are added to yours as bonus levels (aqua on the sheet). Negative stats count too. Its passive runs while it's in the slot. If it has an active skill , that skill joins your ability pool. Put it on any key from your class page (see abilities ). It costs no mana and uses the amulet's own cooldown. Take the amulet off and it leaves the pool. It counts toward a set if it belongs to one. Like all gear it has an item level and does nothing until your character level reaches it (see gear ). The Bonuses page of your journal lists your amulets with their stats and passives. The amulets Amulet Rarity Stats Skill Warrior's Boon Rare +3 Vitality, +3 Might Warrior's Boon (Warriors only) : every hit you take gives +1 armour. Stacks 5 times and fades 5s after the last hit. Eagle Eye Uncommon +2 Precision, +2 Swiftness Eagle Eye (Archers only) : while drawing a bow in mid-air you glide down very slowly (up to 3s per jump). Thief's Gold Mythic +5 Swiftness, +5 Fortune Thief's Gold (Rogues only) : press Sneak in mid-air to double jump. Forbidden Amulet Mythic +10 Arcana, -5 Fortitude, -5 Vitality Forbidden Knowledge (Mages only) : every Arcana ratio on your abilities gets +5 points (40% Arcana counts as 45%). Henrietta's Golden Egg Uncommon +4 Fortune Henrietta's Prophecy (active, 60s cooldown): she clucks, and one of six things comes true. One of them is nothing. Sister Maud's Rosary Uncommon +3 Fortune Maud's Luck : Luck I while worn. A Link of His Chain Uncommon - It does nothing. You kept it anyway. The four class amulets give their stats to anyone, but their skill only works for the class named. Where amulets come from Amulets are never part of ordinary monster loot: The four class amulets (Warrior's Boon, Eagle Eye, Thief's Gold, the Forbidden Amulet) drop from one of the bosses. Henrietta's Golden Egg and Sister Maud's Rosary are rewards from encounters and quests out in the world. A Link of His Chain also comes from a boss. See also Gear · Sets · Abilities · Stats"}];